ratorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238111} {:answer/id #uuid "60b2f004-1b0a-52b0-9b2b-ebdac2ed1485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238905} {:answer/id #uuid "8e197d1b-f46a-5bca-b996-28be90359d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239289} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7cf41d-bf13-5501-a648-617b7fb48f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239447} {:answer/id #uuid "c228a57c-3a58-5cc8-9d7b-d801859aadae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239758} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c03836-aefa-5c2d-a5b3-6de207664cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed315823-9912-53f6-ae66-2685405a1454" :question/math-academy-id "q-103988" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+7=A(x+2)+B(2x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+7 & =A((-2)+2)+B(2(-2)+1) \\\\ 3 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+7 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}+2)+B(2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1) \\\\ 6 & =A\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)} & =\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+7}{(2x+1)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021f6895-433e-546e-8a64-b6694c9f9037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238097} {:answer/id #uuid "17632347-1091-5700-9637-3abb325c6ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238256} {:answer/id #uuid "27e3c466-6f7a-56cd-9dc3-54816a11c3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238402} {:answer/id #uuid "83738b98-16d3-528c-b36c-5c5b3a4b4e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239199} {:answer/id #uuid "f85a7627-23df-52ea-bb89-b64fab1c596d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240252} :answer-field/id #uuid "50cdad8b-c02c-55a4-9c4c-8f85dc88ae18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "992496e6-fae7-5b47-9d13-a4fe5d22fb68" :question/math-academy-id "q-103989" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x-4=A(x-4)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4)-4 & =A(4-4)+B(4) \\\\ 4 & =4B \\\\ 1 & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =A(0-4)+B(0) \\\\ -4 & =-4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34fd9418-01fb-51e1-a770-ae3381d95b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238514} {:answer/id #uuid "59d4ee6b-3131-540a-bbdf-36e3c6e6795f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238828} {:answer/id #uuid "732a4d47-7170-5acd-ac4f-7f9fff58da86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239042} {:answer/id #uuid "7a95ac22-1f38-5655-8cba-2ff63d27f802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239112} {:answer/id #uuid "d33e2234-b01d-5cb8-b1d5-7f5ca23d786e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238828} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21c1770-ea37-5eb9-a28a-c828e1689551" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d0a2cabe-af2e-5f4f-a8d3-773d2a0a8522" :question/math-academy-id "q-10399" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158257b0-40d3-5bb7-bac5-6e36056355d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y=2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238234} {:answer/id #uuid "16004206-d689-5265-94c9-1376fc2a7419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238241} {:answer/id #uuid "41e7778c-2d5c-54b6-9c23-07562072de44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y=2{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238628} {:answer/id #uuid "77b24ff7-d9d9-52ef-a8e8-e1a351aed83f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239088} {:answer/id #uuid "dd57c394-1382-5901-b867-e989f439cece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|+y={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239088} :answer-field/id #uuid "30ba2846-d824-5e4c-aa9d-3131e60267bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf490795-4587-5d21-a17e-b28b1511e0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104058" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\n$$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-1 & ={1}^{2}+C \\\\ 0-1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2258f7-1c89-5ddf-b3f5-be41b960a73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238450} {:answer/id #uuid "783d1f44-8f84-5b7c-88aa-9989c97b87f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2+2x-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239096} {:answer/id #uuid "96fd893a-6a0d-574c-96a1-9c7c9ecdd73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239359} {:answer/id #uuid "c1af356d-96a3-5dbc-a694-254027046ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239753} {:answer/id #uuid "e73db678-16bb-58f9-861b-b9f4c455ff5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{1+x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238450} :answer-field/id #uuid "288ef88e-ee76-5cb6-ba28-c9169ad97783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5f5fc67b-8b37-59a0-97e4-36b78ca0108f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104059" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies $y(-1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{1}{x}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}-Cx},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(-1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{1-(-1)^{2}-C(-1)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{-1}{C} & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(-1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1+x-{x}^{2}}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75be7c90-32ab-5c64-906d-1dd7954d2121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} {:answer/id #uuid "82693f98-0710-5635-895b-2a14d84f4e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239189} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7da46f-6bf6-5179-ad99-a6c9aa84ab9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239852} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ba6483-6e8b-5e55-93bc-ce43b1b86246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+8{x}^{2}-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240204} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c87060-4f09-5b9a-ba36-024d4f3b3691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239071} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9d1e6a-7b24-50e3-9ef5-4dad3d944987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdddaa9b-4174-550d-a7b6-1b8e1a3f2b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-104065" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$ that satisfies $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\pi }^{2}.$ Assume that $0<x<\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x+4{x}^{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral on the right-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{C}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}+{C}_{2}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\pi }^{2}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{\\pi }^{2}+C & ={\\pi }^{2} \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={\\pi }^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+4{x}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25865f2a-e9ab-502a-9acf-7363a0aeafd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{5}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{5}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238382} {:answer/id #uuid "36b1af99-ddb4-5cb0-a70e-0df377a0803c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238527} {:answer/id #uuid "56768848-d4e4-5fe2-9bc0-bb95caa15190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238801} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d0920a-c212-57be-bc60-2bc222f3ffe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240085} {:answer/id #uuid "ee40857a-913f-5d4e-b282-482d4d4ed99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240153} :answer-field/id #uuid "197a188d-df31-5b68-9f39-274a3ad96820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab4c66af-99f0-54ce-8f3e-d6fc71302f94" :question/math-academy-id "q-104119" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle {[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{5}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{5}\\right)]}^{20}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "De Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=20$ and $\\phi =\\frac{2\\theta }{5}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{5}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{5}\\right)]}^{20} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\cdot \\frac{2\\theta }{5})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\cdot \\frac{2\\theta }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "314a8987-0dbc-5852-bbd0-7010c1690aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2-u-v}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238482} {:answer/id #uuid "43bfa857-c999-55d1-942f-f818b8351029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2-u-v}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238643} {:answer/id #uuid "5e82fe8a-7809-5edc-bd45-56e8f05dc50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4-u-v}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238878} {:answer/id #uuid "95201536-8fb2-5b74-8ead-d67378251587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4-u-v}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239346} {:answer/id #uuid "bb55243b-5f88-5a1e-84b1-9044e05df8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2+u-v}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239346} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c2da46-721a-5c72-b1c6-b8ecb6450cd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42a39179-0d26-58fa-a388-5b3e99abc6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104179" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x+y}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1-x-y}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{4-x-y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\frac{4-x-y}{3}⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\frac{4-u-v}{3}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{4-u-v}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1011aa3a-954e-50dc-ae18-549fb483dc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238192} {:answer/id #uuid "37a1c828-f01a-5291-b4c3-a7df666b6ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{x}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238537} {:answer/id #uuid "624d0f96-ecbc-542d-a722-fcec02fdffb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238921} {:answer/id #uuid "77f204bc-bb7d-50f3-9399-6ddab9bf24e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239090} {:answer/id #uuid "cf221d71-61bf-50ec-ba67-26d985655f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238921} :answer-field/id #uuid "25967c04-e2d2-5d17-a034-bddf2421e5de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c5fc6aa-a9f3-5065-aa63-827da84b30b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104191" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\ln ⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =(-1){x}^{-1-1}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3){x}^{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa44a27-ef68-5389-9758-f884a5d93477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2\\sin ⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238277} {:answer/id #uuid "2415dd70-94cd-5924-9d0e-22cf05a020c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}+\\cos ⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238374} {:answer/id #uuid "93cb1ee1-225c-5deb-b3e5-a0bf21607a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\sin ⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239332} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1e2a22-2c42-5e28-96bc-5600c10b0095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}+2\\sin ⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239420} {:answer/id #uuid "b15c3867-b8c6-5277-b1af-d18fc8689a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-2\\cos ⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238277} :answer-field/id #uuid "3465f331-8843-5ef7-b5b2-36db197078df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7aef485-7834-5512-aabf-9615a3c07b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104192" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2\\sin ⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2\\sin ⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}+2\\sin ⁡x) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-1/2-1}+2\\cos ⁡x \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2}+2\\cos ⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3/2}+2\\cos ⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2-1}-2\\sin ⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2\\sin ⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f451759-2d22-5f29-b03c-d4822bf038e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238319} {:answer/id #uuid "43f32b4a-1831-52d5-b099-c5a8aeb262c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238645} {:answer/id #uuid "89405633-2b53-5396-986f-0108dd6a4906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239248} {:answer/id #uuid "d7069a28-3aa6-5b8a-8312-628c6022986c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239934} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd5050-159d-5432-bb68-a3f588368a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239934} :answer-field/id #uuid "2559604b-cd07-5984-a604-420164cbb76e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a63f55a-8c70-5d95-83cc-5a9cc3779061" :question/math-academy-id "q-104241" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{1}{5}r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1}{5}r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals one-fifth the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{(\\frac{1}{5}r)}=5.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055ab99f-f5e5-5862-922f-e80586d6bce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-60x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238125} {:answer/id #uuid "31ab4dc4-f8f6-563e-addf-187d4e82001a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-60x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238488} {:answer/id #uuid "637ebda4-74e8-5447-916e-716524da34c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238928} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4b5e31-c1d2-5fd3-b885-22c78555f74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239130} {:answer/id #uuid "ad353eb8-47c0-5897-baf8-2af62962fe4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239558} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e47e8d-cfec-57b7-884d-14e03e730d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dbc15ca0-e95e-5ce8-90b9-fc14fcd67fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-104294" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}},$ find ${f}^{‴}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-8{x}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-20{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}-20{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}-20{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}-30{x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-2}-30{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{-3}-15{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db5f72c-c336-5047-8e91-472da255ea3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}-6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238175} {:answer/id #uuid "3834310e-b699-5458-b557-3a68b7c58675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+12x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238541} {:answer/id #uuid "426b9489-413a-589b-be16-98a847b71c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}-12x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238634} {:answer/id #uuid "e00fb8fe-6f21-5a36-b905-b4d4e8c37ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}-6x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240015} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a7ff66-9454-5b03-b764-1d181138dccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+6x-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238541} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5ecca4-279a-56bc-8024-89cc5a39f3bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2d13fab-7a95-5b38-9295-5edf9dd98e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-104330" :question/problem "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=9.$ Find $P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=9,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})^{2} & =9{(x-\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right))}^{2} \\\\  & =9{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =9({x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{4}{9}) \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}+12x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=9{x}^{2}+12x+4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3704204a-4676-592b-aaa5-9e2e64d85c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238532} {:answer/id #uuid "40eb5697-4eee-52e7-bd1e-5b0e9560366b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238615} {:answer/id #uuid "61352959-f890-5624-8e94-4220b9515a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238910} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7b1bcf-e526-5c23-b4b0-261977aecea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239111} {:answer/id #uuid "c1109dfa-64db-523d-8c7f-cbed9a7a49b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239742} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5a1c28-fe19-5828-ba03-679dd99175ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "858c0510-e516-5f4a-9f59-887279f0bad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104365" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{ _____ }⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f7e706-6667-560b-bd84-79164bd2ed56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238265} {:answer/id #uuid "485520fe-f7db-5462-90b3-8b9dc00969d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238684} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebd5941-c9bf-5355-a88c-6736db31de31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238730} {:answer/id #uuid "746fc071-c68b-5025-87be-eb8a9b327237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239055} {:answer/id #uuid "fe35b390-cf3c-53c9-b56a-b8e3bb467faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239055} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5d153b-a4b3-593f-9ec7-5127df5989a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10b5a865-9dfd-5753-8931-5381fe6e32af" :question/math-academy-id "q-104442" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{\\sec ⁡x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\sec ⁡x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the derivative and get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{\\pi /3} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sec ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\tan ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1d5393-ef48-5d0c-bb36-8c26d7a03c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238449} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1db26c-93df-591c-b002-b61cfa9afbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238882} {:answer/id #uuid "aea6254b-1a05-521e-9e5c-81502e982634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239576} {:answer/id #uuid "e53a2772-c749-59f9-bd74-79c241dec6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240068} {:answer/id #uuid "f31925be-7a60-54ea-8664-6dc38411577c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238449} :answer-field/id #uuid "84955407-2101-535a-ab84-53af24c52913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1228222-7a6a-50f0-87dd-428760eee980" :question/math-academy-id "q-104497" :question/problem "Given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a solution to the equation $3{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+8x-2=0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+24x-6.$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{\\frac{1}{3}} & 3 & -7 & 8 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\  & 3 & -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{3})(3{x}^{2}-6x+6).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $3{x}^{2}-6x+6=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-2x+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,1\\pm \\text{i}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f068118-69f8-545c-a59c-bbfeb68cc68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238468} {:answer/id #uuid "aff1ff05-43c0-5d47-b081-606201d6e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239583} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c8c053-f62a-5876-84dd-07b34658dade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239593} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f3ad1e-6062-5b73-8e3e-5b0ff92966dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239785} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf0f62f-831c-50ff-abd5-6eca305541e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239857} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a600a63-da52-5853-9d98-985d824bed2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "61dee2fb-b4a6-546d-85eb-f80ded427acb" :question/math-academy-id "q-10457" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6f16ad-810b-5af6-81b5-41c7f62bfc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238607} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c56750-762d-5579-b177-e16cbf7d9a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239470} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a05d0c-6b28-5f09-97e7-d2f65f26077e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239613} {:answer/id #uuid "baeada88-c22e-532f-9d14-7c176a626e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239680} {:answer/id #uuid "cac83832-9ca0-5672-b3e5-6c4b4303ca4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239834} :answer-field/id #uuid "376aad8b-594e-5b5b-8e27-d900a1b34d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99c91096-e517-53ed-91eb-c1bbb59a1a8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104587" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=12({t}^{2}-t)$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}+12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-8{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-8+6)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30453404-690a-5b85-850f-9aaa09a4efe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\ln }^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} {:answer/id #uuid "35cdd507-7f75-51ad-acf4-534f3b62ea96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{2}⁡2+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238522} {:answer/id #uuid "58c144e5-2b4f-5201-b0bb-e144657253c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{3}⁡2-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238819} {:answer/id #uuid "da6b93e1-2f2d-5dd2-9235-e2c75c2150c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239960} {:answer/id #uuid "e08284d3-5a39-5136-ad35-3aadf74bdc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6c211e-df9c-57af-8d94-442d4251996d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7794dd99-ebef-5626-aacd-8b9fce4aa6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104733" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\hspace{1em}1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $\\mathbf{F}=({x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({y}^{2}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{ln}}^{3}2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{ln}}^{3}2-12\\operatorname{ln}2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d4147a-c71a-5c94-85e5-f5d9a8038924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238114} {:answer/id #uuid "27bb17e7-e9f0-57ab-a950-9c30d0a83e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238398} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a11a7-e4b9-5d99-a4e6-6b05e88774ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} {:answer/id #uuid "6d475b88-50f1-508c-94ec-f40b5b005181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238994} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f3259a-1dbd-5b91-9075-78bd6e17f1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afbb94e-3c3c-582d-befe-a39fc2c5fb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5c03f65-0c80-5089-93df-00e7a5829280" :question/math-academy-id "q-104747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}y\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}y\\operatorname{sin}x}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}+\\operatorname{cos}t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}e)-(\\operatorname{sin}0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2899ae-2d92-558c-9a66-8d77817f9bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238161} {:answer/id #uuid "9f89d2e0-0382-543d-9d16-de0848c70470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9(3{t}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239448} {:answer/id #uuid "a90db7c4-73a1-5086-8112-7099c2c7dde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239533} {:answer/id #uuid "d83e6c61-0de6-549e-9ea5-c25dca5221cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5(3{t}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239942} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0f703b-b9d5-5741-9e21-0aabaa02fd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5(3t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239942} :answer-field/id #uuid "578f9bf6-5c48-5135-b648-baf54020ab74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5af44cf5-05c6-5ddb-af78-b148bd717116" :question/math-academy-id "q-104865" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=16{t}^{3}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3{t}^{2}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{3}+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(48{t}^{5}-16{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[8{t}^{6}-4{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-2t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-4+2-2)-0 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-2{t}^{3}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{5}-2+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =5(3{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c262ec4-1077-5eba-960a-29a37e4d49e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238860} {:answer/id #uuid "6548612e-e066-56ee-8307-5a72d4c5b46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238941} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba83d61-3ae8-59ae-a55d-e72b143845a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239122} {:answer/id #uuid "d03f29ea-0758-56ab-b50f-22810c22f6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239877} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9c38fc-ca96-579e-85b3-bed4f624e931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239877} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdce64c6-9a10-5438-b66b-cee58c936ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a0afbf2-8aa8-5003-9b37-01bec551a919" :question/math-academy-id "q-104936" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[-1,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)={t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=5{t}^{2}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2)\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{5}+50{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+20{t}^{2}+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{6}{t}^{6}+10{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{20}{3}{t}^{3}+2t]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6}+10+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{20}{3}+2)-(\\frac{5}{6}-10+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{20}{3}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(25{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}+\\frac{10}{3}{t}^{3}+t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(5+\\frac{10}{3}+1)-(-5-\\frac{10}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{112}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{56}{3}\\right)}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2(5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =10{t}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e0dd75c-56d0-551b-89b7-d9fab453d7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238596} {:answer/id #uuid "45ae8167-66ef-5ab3-b831-89d98c2c2c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238657} {:answer/id #uuid "5068c3ec-ac0b-563a-949b-9a7ae7cf7cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238750} {:answer/id #uuid "6c38c356-e14d-54b7-aadd-cf561a266e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238987} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4563df-7617-52f6-9314-cc7351613997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238987} :answer-field/id #uuid "73b13cb5-e185-51a2-a7cb-7b3fef3143e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4957802d-fba2-523f-8466-48584c16d24e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105107" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{s}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ What is $(\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\mathbf{s}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, multiplying each component of $\\mathbf{r}$ by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{r} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\mathbf{s} & =5\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-7)+2\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5-21+12-2 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a331a5c-83bf-51bb-a8ff-6b21cc037014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3t+5{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238274} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba93c99-a42d-5533-8ae8-22336819e940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5t+3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238446} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebcb3cd-f6e6-5e31-810c-149833a4b0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238879} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4b6f4b-4ed3-5514-8e99-e69c8996f29d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15t+3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239260} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9f55d-358d-5f3b-972b-9633a88a225d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15t+5{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240012}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240012} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd6c944-b116-5f46-b8c9-2e2f39fd3d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c464809f-b653-57ad-9d07-cbfb8f516b25" :question/math-academy-id "q-105400" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=15t+3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t)\\}$ equals $15t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=15t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{2}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(15t+3)\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{15}{4}{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{15}{4}+1)-(\\frac{15}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{f}_{2}(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{2}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}\\cdot {t}^{2}=5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =15t+5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4685a758-8058-59e8-be2d-d72a4eb0fc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238670} {:answer/id #uuid "5dacfce1-68a9-5c38-82ae-51b76dde731a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238872} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7b90df-84fa-5a7d-a3d2-67a93fd997aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238997} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0c2135-f068-52d9-96d9-dbf623cda9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239288} {:answer/id #uuid "d7982561-569e-5b8a-b9c6-caa4a0425f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239288} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b390aba-0d56-5d95-8cc5-d2f376b637b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75f19d0b-76eb-589a-abaa-1f1225a49ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105409" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085714eb-7d9a-5334-9700-9fa44c7f961c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238144} {:answer/id #uuid "56a66d46-1798-5143-a4b3-cf7d0cb3eaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238803} {:answer/id #uuid "97fb3800-58fb-5036-8b4b-a198837450ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239374} {:answer/id #uuid "d80203ed-f8d1-5645-8115-07e9a995066a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239940} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c124e4-910d-5b41-b2e7-5f367bd36fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240104} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5cff9e4-0a90-5443-98d6-89d575a513ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8574b365-99df-52af-a9c4-7d181fbb97bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-105426" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }÷\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }÷\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b374cb36-b270-54a2-9c73-47ae726af5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239620} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0b78c4-2f3a-5fce-823e-d81ae6de6f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239684} {:answer/id #uuid "c32755a8-e99f-5d7a-8200-e7970316f610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239775} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e94380-af17-5752-98a9-8c16b98c6de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239916} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1ca130-7021-5de0-b756-2f3d00100218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239916} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af16464-9867-5fd2-8e7f-875e19c7e569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a91918b-4080-5b4a-a50f-84435b2d1d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105433" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )=4{\\cos }^{2}⁡0=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =1\\cdot 4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674dc164-2a0b-5690-934a-b45e3d221d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238954} {:answer/id #uuid "795b1662-d64e-583f-8a85-af830c4c166e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\frac{8}{5}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239101} {:answer/id #uuid "79f1fdd0-0f04-5c26-b85d-94278dabb270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-\\frac{8}{5},3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239107} {:answer/id #uuid "94c00566-3980-59ae-b26b-83f71eb3e3b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{8}{5},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239342} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dad350-668f-5843-87f0-2de37b360c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-\\frac{8}{5},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239107} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab5ce6a-1dc1-565a-83a9-d50306b768ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ddabafb1-8314-5ad2-b242-235155ac26c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105435" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{12-4x}+\\sqrt{5x+8}$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{12-4x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,4x\\le 12\\,⟹\\,x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+8}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,5x\\ge -8\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3]\\cap [-\\frac{8}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{8}{5},3],\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e4eb1a-f07b-5500-9b6b-bb104419f1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238193} {:answer/id #uuid "1597005a-083a-5b2f-904c-d3601ce7fd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238235} {:answer/id #uuid "189191f2-31ca-5b6a-bb47-311abb8904ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} {:answer/id #uuid "46644984-af18-5419-96cd-d6307758e813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238666} {:answer/id #uuid "94691432-7499-5d85-bd53-7c03102b3e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7bf2326-7e5c-5bac-b442-4f7e564308dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d460b9a-6ed3-5d23-955f-f3e1840b55a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105475" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}8t\\cdot (4t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(32{t}^{2}-16t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-16t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{32}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{32}{3}-8)-0}{(\\frac{16}{3}-4)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}\\cdot (4t+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =8t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-(8t-4)‖ \\\\  & =‖4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨4,4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4,4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{16}=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7e0cda-58ec-5656-85e8-fe04ab7220f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238154} {:answer/id #uuid "100e1515-25fc-57c5-bf1a-0fa837d35049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238191} {:answer/id #uuid "9404f84e-0264-5719-b181-60810ade649d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{6}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239334} {:answer/id #uuid "95060610-8964-5888-b364-695f73fd7b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239343} {:answer/id #uuid "e55dd9b1-d715-59c1-932f-28fd7168e314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238191} :answer-field/id #uuid "933046b4-7b64-59a5-b530-696e16fc151f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e716612d-e557-566c-8ec1-4dfc4dea027a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105478" :question/problem "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(3-18x).$" :question/worked-solution "The logarithmic function $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3-18x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n3-18x>0\\,⟹\\,18x<3\\,⟹\\,x<\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(3-18x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{6}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d086e7-9a00-54ba-a345-73d4f86ae07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238528} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4f0cb7-0675-5d69-8e34-a9010b2385ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238704} {:answer/id #uuid "82b79539-c633-51dc-94f9-26b7bd83dad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239192} {:answer/id #uuid "c976c00b-2eb7-5e8a-a3d2-f96e04c9ee69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239824} {:answer/id #uuid "f7788d12-3ed7-512d-b2fa-46025ca6abef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238528} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f5b310-4ec3-5cee-aa2c-6dc0e3e887bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c26c2acf-e85e-5f98-8d84-d3505431183c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105511" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}7t\\cdot (t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{t}^{2}-14t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}-7{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{14}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =-2t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7t-(-2t+4)‖ \\\\  & =‖9t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)\\cdot (9t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(81{t}^{2}-72t+16)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(27{t}^{3}-36{t}^{2}+16)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207d77ed-be13-5153-afe2-ae80e5b7f112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238337} {:answer/id #uuid "28105387-c390-577f-9ff0-c4baeace7982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238404} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bb9480-a81f-5b1c-a3e4-9b258924585f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239622} {:answer/id #uuid "e275e549-1a9e-5716-9267-52c2998cfc50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240046} {:answer/id #uuid "eccf72c3-ca4d-588a-9a06-99d2e7feb952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238337} :answer-field/id #uuid "0856888b-fde6-55ae-8b77-4199daefdce1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "812f95db-2c0e-5010-aa63-2f9280fe2856" :question/math-academy-id "q-10564" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2-\\frac{x}{2}+2{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x.$ Rearranging this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cot }^{2}⁡x & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2-\\frac{x}{2}+2{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2-\\frac{x}{2}+2({\\csc }^{2}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{x}{2}+2{\\csc }^{2}⁡x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{x}{2}+2{\\csc }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00280b32-71c4-532d-9ed6-1f60c4e33219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238080} {:answer/id #uuid "74c95bec-b27e-512a-91ca-00e32817e792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{y}^{2}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239063} {:answer/id #uuid "8c454451-3ad3-58d7-8c60-1573ad383c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239274} {:answer/id #uuid "9abca964-a6b5-5813-b91d-e7f5a62b70d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}(y-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239402} {:answer/id #uuid "a894c8bb-9fce-5e1b-8bfa-f4623e8555ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}(y+2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239402} :answer-field/id #uuid "3957ab7a-7e2e-56d9-acb9-f5d7285da366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "45c07a5a-8062-5571-b180-2aa5c3573b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-105645" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=⟨-{x}^{2},\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}⟩$ and $f(x,y)={y}^{2},$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,2y⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})=-2x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(-2x+y)+⟨-{x}^{2},\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,2y⟩ \\\\  & =-2x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}+{y}^{3} \\\\  & =2{y}^{3}-2x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}(y-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3785aacb-aa32-5962-a866-9d43a4cfac4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238536} {:answer/id #uuid "408a6bbe-de4c-528f-9c22-a753e0340e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238614} {:answer/id #uuid "919fcc80-0550-5c80-868e-27476b4f2ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239316} {:answer/id #uuid "e88f4cd7-7623-529f-8b35-2234ea398a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240102} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5ce10e-0406-52ea-830a-4d41511a41fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240268} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e19524f-666f-593d-b519-1f4421ef3662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb2247f7-5823-54d1-ba7e-d63c871dccbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-105662" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+4{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=2X,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}Y,\\,z=5Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,\\frac{1}{2}Y,5Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1639f667-cc0c-541f-beb3-df7075bcd242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238243} {:answer/id #uuid "746766cf-a9af-578f-8dc5-0e36b21b4e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239054} {:answer/id #uuid "7681ef8d-3f67-572c-af5f-8f55918debf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239078} {:answer/id #uuid "7de0f017-aa4f-55fa-b7f6-e4e3b289359f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239147} {:answer/id #uuid "e7823a5e-3336-5cc7-868c-2023128957eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{k}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239147} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2209b7-9fdc-539d-9358-25e617c5c773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b3b1abb-e804-5079-8fb6-32a843b8454e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105668" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=3{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k},$ what is $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{curl} \\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{z}^{2})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-0)\\mathbf{i}-(0-6z)\\mathbf{j}+(0-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d650c1-149f-5757-9587-2d05e7bdf855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238205} {:answer/id #uuid "5335d16f-a84e-5b3e-b354-de02ea150c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238772} {:answer/id #uuid "64681e8d-39d1-5faf-ac7f-a604c349e91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238936} {:answer/id #uuid "940d2c4d-f823-5543-85bc-88a7957dace4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239336} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9d2f46-656b-5f67-8740-d57524fe0094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239840} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb055678-80b5-562a-bc4a-fd37fcc7ea2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f79178f2-ea4e-568f-a26a-8eda65a5b236" :question/math-academy-id "q-10567" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{\\cot }^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =3x-2\\pi ,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ) & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{\\cot }^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7+7{\\cot }^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7[1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi )])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+7(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))+C \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133c1b20-5150-50da-8523-674470a0cfe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238215} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8be352-8015-5be2-b8df-9a4f3ad0aa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238865} {:answer/id #uuid "82de7a70-0733-5cde-9eac-f208a1690d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239194} {:answer/id #uuid "82fbfc1b-0c81-5da1-927a-375691efc6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239197} {:answer/id #uuid "853fbdfa-bdb1-5373-adfe-5cf0720fa54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239197} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42bc9e6-d9aa-54be-b2ee-e7d0d024b8b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8f1a0a3-0b71-52cd-b327-6c0ec92e34be" :question/math-academy-id "q-105672" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}={e}^{y+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $(2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{curl} \\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {e}^{y+z} & 1 & xyz\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y+z})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y+z})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(xz-0)\\mathbf{i}-(yz-{e}^{y+z})\\mathbf{j}+(0-{e}^{y+z})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({e}^{y+z}-yz)\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{y+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F})(2,1,-1) & =2(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({e}^{1+(-1)}-1(-1))\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{1+(-1)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56985235-fa3e-5355-b5e3-46e01ac2a247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi )-{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238802} {:answer/id #uuid "6987c4ae-ab66-56c9-8d35-74bdb216be38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi )+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238971} {:answer/id #uuid "d13bd42c-6107-59af-8803-31227c827109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi )-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239889} {:answer/id #uuid "e070939b-4357-5295-9190-1c5ff7d82693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi )-{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240018} {:answer/id #uuid "e69b1fa3-ba07-57cc-8ea6-7cdd8b8fecc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi )-{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240081} :answer-field/id #uuid "60df668c-def0-5d48-85b2-7b3977057599" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d63e7c45-202f-53e4-8cfc-4ac24fd29e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-10568" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (9{\\tan }^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )-5{x}^{4}+9)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the identity  $1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =(5x+\\pi ),$ we get \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )={\\sec }^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (9{\\tan }^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )-5{x}^{4}+9)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (9[1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )]-5{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (9{\\sec }^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )-5{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi ))-5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi )-{x}^{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "356837f8-df61-5c09-935e-a79420904ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238520} {:answer/id #uuid "656e9149-b61d-5692-8f1b-4a048892b221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238942} {:answer/id #uuid "9238f92b-156c-55c8-847d-2be19a29797e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239320} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1c851a-1e54-5ec2-8ad0-f8a67680be19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239438} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd45d8-0755-589b-8fd9-a27a1b0014f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239438} :answer-field/id #uuid "850005a1-194a-5d38-ae0b-5ace34bdea7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa936d4a-99bb-5dd2-8408-bca10f4f0e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105689" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ above parametrizes the ellipsoid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+25{z}^{2}=1.\n$$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following substitution:\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=\\frac{1}{5}Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Cartesian equation of our ellipsoid, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}+{Z}^{2}=1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin in $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =1.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(X,Y,Z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nX=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,Z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,2Y,\\frac{1}{5}Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1be6b6-827b-556d-8966-251bd12e605a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239012} {:answer/id #uuid "70f82a1e-53c6-524e-a7ff-64808ed7a29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239025} {:answer/id #uuid "72927edc-c1a9-52f3-922f-1b7e560c12d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239036} {:answer/id #uuid "b3022f7a-5df9-5940-a134-308e53cdeba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239615} {:answer/id #uuid "f10fb272-948a-5737-a6ea-dd39ac144715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239036} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f87b35-0579-5bff-b5e2-925e05121692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "edc4b606-5631-55c8-88e7-f82312ee7534" :question/math-academy-id "q-105792" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\times (2F-G)$ at the point $(0,-1,2)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i+yz\\,j,\\,(∇\\times G)(x,y,z)=z\\,i+(x+2y)\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (2F-G) & =∇\\times (2F)+∇\\times (-G) \\\\  & =2∇\\times F-∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {e}^{x} & yz & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})]k \\\\  & =(0-y)i-(0-0)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =-y\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (2F-G) & =2∇\\times F-∇\\times G \\\\  & =2[-y\\,i]-[z\\,i+(x+2y)\\,k] \\\\  & =-2y\\,i-z\\,i-(x+2y)\\,k \\\\  & =-(2y+z)\\,i-(x+2y)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,-1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (2F-G))(0,-1,2) & =-(2(-1)+2)\\,i-(0+2(-1))\\,k \\\\  & =-(-2+2)\\,i-(0-2)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115898a8-f7c2-5178-a532-6690f2207aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238196} {:answer/id #uuid "27408130-61ae-55e3-b517-9a41491d429c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238395} {:answer/id #uuid "33dc4908-05c5-5598-831a-6317deaee379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238505} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd4500e-8c49-56dd-af08-b21d5743637a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,i+j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238595} {:answer/id #uuid "db8f1b89-de4c-514b-b218-524c95bdca94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-5\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239970} :answer-field/id #uuid "cda6e655-7e57-5d19-9e6b-f59539fe3df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa63662a-86b1-5a38-9365-b4edf911d944" :question/math-academy-id "q-105799" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\times (F+3G)$ at the point $(0,1,-2)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\,i+z\\,j+{x}^{2}\\,k,\\,(∇\\times G)(x,y,z)=-3{x}^{2}\\,j-k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F+3G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times (3G) \\\\  & =∇\\times F+3∇\\times G\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {y}^{2} & z & {x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-1)i-(2x-0)j+(0-2y)k \\\\  & =-i-2x\\,j-2y\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F+3G) & =∇\\times F+3∇\\times G \\\\  & =-i-2x\\,j-2y\\,k+3[-3{x}^{2}\\,j-k] \\\\  & =-i-2x\\,j-2y\\,k-9{x}^{2}\\,j-3k \\\\  & =-i-(9{x}^{2}+2x)\\,j-(2y+3)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F+3G))(0,1,-2) & =-i-(9(0)^{2}+2(0))\\,j-(2(1)+3)\\,k \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5033ae-e9b3-5d47-b65e-9b2087d2b8ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-3,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238440} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7e1336-d218-5bab-af5f-5db4a021be48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-4,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238836} {:answer/id #uuid "adbfa1f7-56c2-56f2-8e8d-059420e4d718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239562} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf0d2de-df09-508e-92e8-67f3c6320633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239693} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb316d6-5409-5a1d-a42e-5287e7b1d5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238440} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd7c6d3-a5bf-5348-a990-e86743a44d17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ccf254f7-98f8-509b-bf14-7e75e519ef48" :question/math-academy-id "q-105960" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,0,z⟩$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n∇\\times F=⟨0,0,-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩,\\,∇f=⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},0⟩,\n$$\n\ncalculate $∇\\times (f\\,F)$ at the point $(1,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} & 0 \\\\ y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & 0 & z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}(z)-0(0)]i-[\\frac{1}{y}(z)-0(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]j+[\\frac{1}{y}(0)-\\left(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\right)(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]k \\\\  & =-\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{z}{y}\\,j+\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}},-\\frac{z}{y},\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}⟨0,0,-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩+⟨-\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}},-\\frac{z}{y},\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}⟩+⟨-\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}},-\\frac{z}{y},\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}},-\\frac{z}{y},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(1,1,3) & =⟨-\\frac{1(3)}{(1)^{2}},-\\frac{3}{1},0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1179ddc8-9d2b-5f37-858d-a4af5d525038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238199} {:answer/id #uuid "5c77ee41-eb12-5d5d-aee0-f73a63e3d59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238864} {:answer/id #uuid "868b4972-5724-52d5-879b-b7ce9a27b554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239226} {:answer/id #uuid "e91810a1-61c1-5eba-91a0-ed9b1bf5ef6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240107} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3e3abf-1c31-5d04-ae31-72a0f1e5bc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238864} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f97f707-7649-51de-a352-c860d9645f7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "85bc2cb1-6e4a-5b13-b0c1-a6335dc66a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-106300" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275cbc97-498b-541f-b490-b72a4ab58e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238396} {:answer/id #uuid "74ffffaa-f33b-547e-93c3-da339f101aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239065} {:answer/id #uuid "97d904bb-b2da-58f1-80e3-6c632732919d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239372} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7be160-4c62-5f6d-9a5c-7c8834e48bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239399} {:answer/id #uuid "b456abd5-51b5-5847-b511-91f87dd5e414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239631} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2a1dfb-0aab-5eed-a08a-01fe0d294c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d3351acb-0711-5b2c-8212-2f838f3d3d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106402" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea3bc202dd0a40cfe096fb4170331fed39de28870aa355c776fd82de3b5db26b.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/74/74714486ad225aba7111329be0c3853b7b922a852df450b057632e73fce5be6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154230c4-e5cc-5eb7-a10c-06c3c17162be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238230} {:answer/id #uuid "283b8e16-5fca-5dfa-9a88-d69fd0c9c6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238407} {:answer/id #uuid "47ee14be-4f73-52f9-be6e-e1119afc63d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238680} {:answer/id #uuid "c72d8441-2566-5fb6-a259-a23948627804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239810} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa97086-0b06-56cd-a813-1ae30f693d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238407} :answer-field/id #uuid "2104c834-96d2-54e9-ad19-6dc6e3eb1a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9cfc762-69e6-5d11-8bd6-6402006ff4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106403" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b344621d5500d3253295c970b0a7557b80f4d2cbfa66c17ba6f8868c1424c08.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/9e/9e236591e21403bb739dafa68a665c8a10eed0e9e8ac104e7b39431f02329c17.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b913d54-324a-5ad8-aa62-3a070b030f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238850} {:answer/id #uuid "c3097672-8b26-512d-acb0-da5b48991b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239772} {:answer/id #uuid "ca724085-3fcc-56b7-9d3e-9b5df0e5c9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239833} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aa8820-6119-5eac-8a02-513174a82ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240049} {:answer/id #uuid "e95fd9a6-9c15-593c-a235-3b994fbad558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240109} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1883e4-6fef-59d0-bdd7-282f4291d66b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fb99db2-aa7f-56fb-932b-79345c0c1bd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106404" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbaad877ff577039a288011f904d856bec1417d995113c4c137f7ac536d44968.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5a/5a028284d29850a9561a589c0e9fbf85e2e10e4e7a23d4ab46a765f492955b4f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "014cd8f5-8e05-5ca6-95a0-18e9e5d8c81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241197} {:answer/id #uuid "04f9de1f-4f25-5b06-9fe7-00d80cb7f25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241220} {:answer/id #uuid "05be06f8-b710-5b7c-9856-516ebac1062f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17