b/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247852} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf8e15f-3174-57fb-b8e4-5d360ff846f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248257} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8775a6-08c9-5e68-8ded-0ebea2805076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248846} {:answer/id #uuid "a3358706-6bfa-540b-8aac-27669d842b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248257} :answer-field/id #uuid "6296fb51-a261-5315-98e2-bafc87eb4fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4d360a8-fe79-5362-9972-fed2a1994303" :question/math-academy-id "q-114074" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012323fc-4e7b-5948-bd33-ac6535cdacea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247423} {:answer/id #uuid "275108dc-ba21-516f-8c42-bd8bf8d71064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247772} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab5218b-e36e-59ff-8b03-f71a3f5c4374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247944} {:answer/id #uuid "82b6c265-2f3d-5dbe-b622-87847517b9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248580} {:answer/id #uuid "afcf5a5d-6b1c-5e07-b91a-41eca607a863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249002} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f84de4-1a81-5ce2-ac8e-5901c4a2a352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c216ad6d-32f4-5235-ae6f-6f4e1e6db054" :question/math-academy-id "q-114080" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b573ef-1007-53ad-a1f4-f1bbb6a97f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247434} {:answer/id #uuid "21345a50-5601-54ac-b6b8-8854f3bcf25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247719} {:answer/id #uuid "21932059-2779-56bb-aff8-46d2133806aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247722} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ca1c00-7abb-53af-b061-a218e5f95af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249025} {:answer/id #uuid "fe379fad-f57a-50e1-ad28-7deffbfbf656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247434} :answer-field/id #uuid "20529d1c-5c66-5379-87b8-bf5bcb3ca23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d206bd2a-2b8b-589e-945a-46c1dd7ed6d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114084" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31bd6778-18b0-5ff5-8b99-5a553e76b73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247877} {:answer/id #uuid "4a97465c-2eb1-5200-9609-a38e8e5419dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248089} {:answer/id #uuid "5170f02c-e217-5a70-9d06-19689007bd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248155} {:answer/id #uuid "70c0b236-95a4-5fec-9b61-50baea92bf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248412} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0c8113-6440-560f-bdcf-41c0564064c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248412} :answer-field/id #uuid "c16c266a-b08e-5ef0-9fc4-f4220c1b8349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a79dcdc-c1c8-5a71-9d84-cbbd212d39b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114156" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ace781-d274-5e56-a670-5a4a11cc396e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{x}^{3}\\text{ arcsin }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247581} {:answer/id #uuid "5692d53f-e590-5c4b-b3cf-b26aac77396a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+7\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248210} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc18efd-3204-5471-89ef-885c34415cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+7\\text{ arcsin }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249128} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dad63e-a18d-5765-8de3-fdf0ca004d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{x}^{3}\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249295} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4b608f-ffe9-5210-b69f-f21778e1a218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-7\\text{ arctan }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248210} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b1cbd54-3439-59c4-a3d8-a241f241223c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eb0e1654-81f3-51ff-a1ad-22ecef08f60d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114164" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+7(\\operatorname{arcsec} x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+7\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263a6676-bcc6-56f2-bf75-5b204a0665c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247761} {:answer/id #uuid "8afc0f1b-1e0c-5d92-a062-0d61fc9caadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248663} {:answer/id #uuid "9369dced-f14b-556e-945e-9f6e682f7d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248734} {:answer/id #uuid "945bb0e9-9cf8-5d4f-a7ae-aab444d3de81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248744} {:answer/id #uuid "f074aa68-1eb8-5f6d-8644-d4e407e90daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249535} :answer-field/id #uuid "916b2a0c-4759-5859-ac15-e71d9797d8fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca385184-ef23-5e09-850b-a334c3c5908a" :question/math-academy-id "q-114168" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =3(0-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504396e6-9557-5851-9ee4-f27af084185e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248142} {:answer/id #uuid "79318c76-7a71-5d7a-afa5-ddf2fbef3f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248486} {:answer/id #uuid "95bd5fa3-7fb6-5fb6-9a47-e3ebad507ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248758} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d97b95-1400-5b69-8155-135a3c945e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248932} {:answer/id #uuid "e461ba2d-a5a0-56df-80c7-b457c4ca6df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248486} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0f75b0-549c-53e6-8de6-b8ddc9e7f993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4ab15c5-bd19-5639-9b61-2d4a68d67bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-114175" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{1}^{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3)+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d777e7-64e4-53c8-a58b-4109b26ec30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247779} {:answer/id #uuid "4725e1f3-3bac-54c9-976b-a4be3acdee45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248055} {:answer/id #uuid "5f842a7a-96b0-5892-bbf8-16bcb12e7b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248289} {:answer/id #uuid "ac802b62-ee40-527f-a541-bc50021df648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248964} {:answer/id #uuid "c7af75c9-f04f-5c0c-b971-fa76073ccb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248964} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6e76fc-ebfb-5b23-9799-3f3027022a53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9199096c-1056-59b2-bc8e-bf89ee34e2ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-114181" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-1+6}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)+5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573f0bf8-8c3a-5fd3-87b0-1f98d36645e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248215} {:answer/id #uuid "7df83fc4-3a1c-5b4e-9334-3a07c3e58e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x-5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248529} {:answer/id #uuid "857ce7ea-a4b4-5485-b3d8-49d024d15f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x+5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248613} {:answer/id #uuid "881c84d2-e368-5509-bb5a-80ca75c9ca6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248639} {:answer/id #uuid "994d053b-d19e-559c-a29d-2a8054864f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248529} :answer-field/id #uuid "7223fe64-f45f-5bb2-b016-eb100aa48182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d5fd5d6-3989-51af-a4dc-33f4a2f20e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-114183" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}+5-5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5({x}^{2}+1)-5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5x-5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454a889b-721a-50b6-97c3-f47be3defb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248046} {:answer/id #uuid "824243f4-5ca1-536a-9f8d-289db11728a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248577} {:answer/id #uuid "86b2b588-7b9d-56fe-a27a-0e737e5e0cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248627} {:answer/id #uuid "97a50aac-2235-5530-bca7-b5df0d59c773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248775} {:answer/id #uuid "a81bfa86-2d4c-5595-afcd-7b763ef010ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248627} :answer-field/id #uuid "c544ada9-fdbb-5a15-af23-b77ee756bc20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7663d90a-9599-5f84-ad9c-e23f99291791" :question/math-academy-id "q-114184" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}+4-4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({x}^{2}+1)-4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9fbe19-cf60-531c-baf0-c504de2ac80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247524} {:answer/id #uuid "19c56598-e8ce-5d22-8ffa-5f30dd11841a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247660} {:answer/id #uuid "57c92f5d-6664-5cf7-9fc7-0643b248d475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248221} {:answer/id #uuid "5d314c53-79f8-54f8-b84b-eae20722ae51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248272} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cc8340-3010-5529-9554-55c7d0dc7818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247660} :answer-field/id #uuid "366d90ae-e6be-58b7-a32f-61ddab4f0f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "646baf8c-2fc5-5cdc-90e1-2b00b50de1be" :question/math-academy-id "q-114202" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-3{y}^{-2})\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-3{y}^{-2})\\text{d}y & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{y}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1}-3(-{y}^{-1}){|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =2(-1-(-2))+3(\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{(-2)}) \\\\  & =2(-1+2)+3(-1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2(1)+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022d7b7b-070a-51f5-bd49-94b4db34c49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247437} {:answer/id #uuid "2e34cf00-1e95-5551-a888-a986e9043bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247834} {:answer/id #uuid "76108c8b-8f42-5f4a-9dd2-e847f09e6e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248463} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfdc77f-bbbe-57f4-9329-f07738aa11a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248678} {:answer/id #uuid "c6440ae3-1132-5195-b3a2-356c7752e48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247834} :answer-field/id #uuid "eede1f3b-85f2-5008-b473-3fcd59ae080b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "872e80da-db87-5cbe-89ef-83c58a2b3950" :question/math-academy-id "q-114289" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{3}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′} & ={3}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bc501d-4124-5179-a51b-315ac85a1b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248757} {:answer/id #uuid "9f69fd84-e05c-5d24-ae8e-9d8f383459c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248843} {:answer/id #uuid "dea80f9c-54f5-5396-b7ef-3e0d29ed1c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249399} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7731aa-f29b-5e06-aa8d-a528db3cff78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249627} {:answer/id #uuid "ff931226-3ee2-5a37-af2b-7544f41260e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{8}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248757} :answer-field/id #uuid "75347d01-2014-547c-a5ad-13e10a343907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b188b68-b90e-5d2a-a750-30bfecf83280" :question/math-academy-id "q-114299" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3878ae79-1e5e-59bf-8bb6-ae0cf3aaa4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247928} {:answer/id #uuid "5f75b493-3947-527e-a588-60cab80d2be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248287} {:answer/id #uuid "74e4c221-78c2-5702-af4f-442e99facd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248451} {:answer/id #uuid "cef45258-9cb1-562e-a7e4-faf62bbc7406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249259} {:answer/id #uuid "e0dd153a-e09b-5898-9188-70f04b5e8044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247928} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b71088-363e-519b-b126-45df5c59e3e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "40079a0f-e072-5ffd-a039-30e177ed76fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-114304" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d87cf2-ba4e-5ee2-b888-16334d204447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247661} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfa8fa7-c8a9-5491-a85f-a85434d1131e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247678} {:answer/id #uuid "5b42bf75-0d9b-5855-aac9-9999790d5338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248251} {:answer/id #uuid "a03cb483-a296-5946-8a8d-952eac55cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248855} {:answer/id #uuid "aedb9126-7c6d-5c55-ad4b-869a319638e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247661} :answer-field/id #uuid "0416b7bd-da37-58c6-8855-48ba00ca405e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "edc6fd62-64ca-5ae7-b219-55728bf85a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114306" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0] \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2621a51d-6ea2-5c0c-b0e8-ef9a5309fa09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247760} {:answer/id #uuid "679c8c73-db45-58a5-8311-c18d1aa796b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248353} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bb94e3-7ab2-5067-a9f7-907364396e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248924} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6ee47a-69b2-58b2-804c-4404e96412f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249242} {:answer/id #uuid "e05fabc1-bec3-5016-a5ff-3dafd2caba5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249410}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248353} :answer-field/id #uuid "580bbc55-c5cf-5ded-aa0f-fb666956d730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6eaf6d0-3cca-5bcd-87e4-c38716de9570" :question/math-academy-id "q-114313" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c3019c-5b9e-5399-b786-04437705e092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248061} {:answer/id #uuid "6f81ffd7-1892-5f9b-9320-925504438589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248407} {:answer/id #uuid "89d313a8-f000-577f-a816-a34a6622496f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248650} {:answer/id #uuid "a7092c76-1bc1-50c0-b6c7-d05f207eda48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248909} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f08285-93fd-5b63-ade9-e4f908688cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248061} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1b567b-6514-5d16-ba8c-83023f80f2da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "062977fc-037b-5a67-9a07-c0d038a63f3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114318" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\n$$ to express this as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)] \\\\  & =-[1-\\sqrt{2}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e72326f-b37b-556e-9771-d69f461e0b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248129} {:answer/id #uuid "5979c57e-1a61-504f-9748-c153fbd57b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248234} {:answer/id #uuid "82511b39-0f0e-5f20-82f8-c82314f7aca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248578} {:answer/id #uuid "941618c9-aa44-56dd-b179-4d773a5937a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248740} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f13165-476e-58f7-beb6-a83b3ddfdb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248234} :answer-field/id #uuid "8956bd65-6e03-5145-875e-cd65f5f6055b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b71aa220-d93a-5e59-b207-8dade0373ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114322" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi }+{\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )}{4}+\\frac{0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0)}{4}] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{16}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0)+\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d83e583-d218-50f4-abb7-474c6d01c304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247546} {:answer/id #uuid "3d047995-440b-51a6-837d-6a9c0a9dea33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247961} {:answer/id #uuid "4217347b-9b32-5330-8c51-bcd370f084c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248013} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7776ae-27e1-5fb2-91d8-1d3d11b460fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248680} {:answer/id #uuid "aea67226-a698-5829-a7e0-c9089694e776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248013} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff430e6-479c-5fff-ae3c-19a79a5cfe3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37affd24-3b4f-536c-8927-723157249685" :question/math-academy-id "q-114328" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{9}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+\\frac{1}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77d8920c-9eae-5d0e-8971-ca7e7b55533e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e-3}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248477} {:answer/id #uuid "7b577e2d-1a9b-598e-a5bb-8e6d22918919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{e+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248506} {:answer/id #uuid "82f9258b-b72f-566d-a506-e8c7094ed4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248582} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d80631-c8d5-5425-9e65-8029a411c1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249042} {:answer/id #uuid "fb407c98-f1d8-5183-b7e1-e23543be95c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{e+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249042} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf81a2af-d8b3-5358-9ec2-8ae5505c5bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e406eb1a-a97b-5da3-8a7a-44bb2a8c8ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-114329" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}+3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3}+3{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3}-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =-3[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3(1/3)}-0\\cdot {e}^{-3\\cdot 0}]-[{e}^{-3(1/3)}-{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =-3[\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-0]-[{e}^{-1}-1] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}-\\frac{1}{e}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "353c38c6-0699-5d31-a3a7-681d96b0021d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247901} {:answer/id #uuid "5507088f-1a8b-5d15-a203-9e83009f5db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248191} {:answer/id #uuid "85d825e8-4b77-570d-9579-44732d982d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248617} {:answer/id #uuid "c26ec3ba-1125-5219-b27d-d1384ee85f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249151} {:answer/id #uuid "d210b2f5-3730-5d88-a77c-6afcdc80ecd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248617} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cbe6e3-ee8a-56d6-bba3-4dcd2dab931c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ba270d9-6da5-5830-bec9-26eadbb91904" :question/math-academy-id "q-114330" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{5}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =(0-0)+\\frac{1}{5}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321312ca-35e6-590b-b440-c12b3be53dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{66}{13},-\\frac{18}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247881} {:answer/id #uuid "73f9e2ae-8b3b-58ab-9adc-90853b9cafce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{90}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248444} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcc3641-ada3-55ad-bd99-688a6222a88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248510} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb2bdd5-d7c7-5d30-bb92-8a47f30a62f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},-\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248809} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f270dd-8b9b-593e-b900-f2f6c240780a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249594} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d014b53-8f67-50f0-a715-6786d9eb85c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1e7059ea-e4f5-5468-9ed3-af001f239b6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114336" :question/problem "![](images/69/69e06e1b9b72e3a4c1fc2e7f2fcd85aa4aa8b34e4fa2f4f5aebb8d1b10474415.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} {:answer/id #uuid "14581a73-ac53-505c-8447-0394a875fcd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247608} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb5a940-7e41-5de0-9f76-33adbc3d81da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248386} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6d5bb5-c38f-58f9-bdcf-296b1625b1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249109} {:answer/id #uuid "de549bfd-d213-57c1-ad88-fadc596d833b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d3e168-c279-50d0-a119-0930eb63b6db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd8e8e07-0b88-58f7-806a-13412093afb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114345" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf8bb55-4020-5f0b-bd59-42ee32fbb771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247677} {:answer/id #uuid "506a9fe3-70ee-5719-9e0a-dcce648d0473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248144} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf2ce60-0e78-5b08-b8b7-0a60d6e30d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248821} {:answer/id #uuid "bb02feef-1bc2-517b-96ff-29402a331b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249094} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb3d233-8364-50ab-b259-e0a0966e7b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},-\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248821} :answer-field/id #uuid "2932adcc-4eb8-504c-836b-faa4fbe63c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3954acb1-fda8-5e85-a442-60c3272bf340" :question/math-academy-id "q-114346" :question/problem "![](images/df/df3838ae44d1477a4a0de303972cd26a2ceaad9b213f1d8e4be3a53bd453aeb2.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3x & =2 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=3x+2$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $3,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3x+2 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 10x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0)$ is  $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce54910-1244-5386-86d8-f09d91856276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247960} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6a67d4-b82b-5092-bade-43eff2cd1bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248536} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e5e077-c051-52e8-9324-38476f689c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249076} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ca5e2c-1d09-5ba6-bac7-e091b9182a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{5},\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249285} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e2987c-b486-55c8-9dc4-132ab31c0731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248536} :answer-field/id #uuid "c816ccdc-152d-585a-8fbf-41891b81689b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "855df716-3231-50d2-97cf-6767626cecd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-114349" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba46b4f3f458a6be13ea01ca1351658c102732db94e22725890afaaade6fc904.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-10x & =5 \\\\ 5y & =10x+5 \\\\ y & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=2x+1$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x+1 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3)$ is  $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53a5ce15-8545-5507-9875-4858166892ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248177} {:answer/id #uuid "6e89cbc9-5e23-5e0b-b9bf-f6d1932ca856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248404} {:answer/id #uuid "89ea9e1a-7382-5ca5-a09c-8ea7c839b012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248651} {:answer/id #uuid "de32f839-bded-50ef-83aa-fd6cbcc36cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249394} {:answer/id #uuid "f157fb56-50a8-596b-b6d5-8d047e671068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249541} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e45400-7471-5bcc-aded-c65faddf5ecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2171a240-7846-596a-ba37-d0a6bdd37eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114350" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({\\csc }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192f0711-d55c-54c1-ac3b-10bbb9d90325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247650} {:answer/id #uuid "2420a00b-8993-51c9-a7ec-8f593f1087a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247739} {:answer/id #uuid "55c77c69-7f9d-53ec-bc57-775c7a8eb546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248199} {:answer/id #uuid "570c35db-46b1-5a19-9b59-605bcf9b8053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248213} {:answer/id #uuid "8b235cea-ca61-5478-8e9a-f1b19691f30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248213} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a356fc-a06e-5ab6-aa77-918e09dee889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9130ee0d-24eb-5b04-a0dc-d2670f4f9892" :question/math-academy-id "q-114364" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+3x & =-6 \\\\ 2y & =-3x-6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/715e1292edaf78979f6b9ae36586a8196dcc30e865915e7eefba2360281f85ec.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3$ and passes through the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{5}{6}+3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-2)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029577c8-4387-5dce-abc1-e362769c0f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247442} {:answer/id #uuid "4e18de69-f618-587b-a2ec-5220370f7b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248124} {:answer/id #uuid "8d733b4b-1dca-53a4-a8f8-a44dd6bcbc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248683} {:answer/id #uuid "a03c6caa-d5e0-51d2-ab76-a5150a905444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248854} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf847c0-a882-5640-8efa-f13f377ac775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248854} :answer-field/id #uuid "e155ce82-9147-55f3-82af-e32fec80f54d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "590fb4d1-46ef-5eb8-ba9c-0c9e6a8189ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-114429" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-{\\sec }^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-{\\sec }^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)({\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)] \\\\  & =-[2-\\sqrt{2}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a05c2fc-f94d-5489-955e-6e93003cb216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247662} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dc3121-d85d-555d-95fa-e1f130a553c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248887} {:answer/id #uuid "a75bb911-b1a2-59de-ac5b-2d5b63e7d0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248911} {:answer/id #uuid "c53da061-88b8-5d38-9620-69fbe5f388d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249176} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bfdf52-8608-5e41-81ac-9c0e93199b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248911} :answer-field/id #uuid "a980c72c-15a0-5296-ad73-7e85baf7f354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3841fdbd-8341-5e24-9f59-0d6b99ab3106" :question/math-academy-id "q-114441" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\cot }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x.$ Rearranging this gives\n\n$$\n{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\cot }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}({\\csc }^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-x){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =[-0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c892137-db18-57ab-a0c4-ee121e607cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{3}}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247538} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2a7067-db8e-5098-bfe6-d1f90479e95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248104} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0a2abd-f164-5bc3-8dd3-b337d3b3b315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248389} {:answer/id #uuid "ad009786-2f7e-5893-ae43-3dd8c76182ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248969} {:answer/id #uuid "f843f5bd-ea3e-5dce-a8a3-d0d60b0e0281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247538} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0563b6-4107-5961-bf6f-8fdb92fa27b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ae308dc-8f77-58de-a2c1-ae20d02ecf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-114454" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2+3{x}^{2}+2{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2+3{x}^{2}+2{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{\\csc }^{2}⁡x+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{x}^{3}){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}-[-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{3}] \\\\  & =-0+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}+2(1)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{64} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{3}}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429c4e06-3048-55b1-b208-72019f1b0474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248017} {:answer/id #uuid "7e54eb59-7b2b-5295-a85f-69bab12eefd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248535} {:answer/id #uuid "8021e056-c15c-5db2-b077-c41b6d899f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248549} {:answer/id #uuid "848e9fc1-831c-51f0-9f79-1b100b19ee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248600} {:answer/id #uuid "b94c2031-9662-5675-9f0a-6203b77aab15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248535} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0cd4974-971c-56d8-b46a-a70cd95372ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a520c33d-1aa5-5864-8fa9-daf66894b11e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114469" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})={\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x &= \\int \\frac{1}{[{\\tan }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+1]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &= \\int \\frac{1}{{\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &= \\int \\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &= \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\text{d}x \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17074cab-3459-5914-a6fd-ff9fa965d1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247633} {:answer/id #uuid "81a424ea-895b-5655-9e86-96e2b6d006e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248572} {:answer/id #uuid "ada25af1-a6cd-5ea9-ab11-56079d625a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248975} {:answer/id #uuid "d2346634-424b-580d-9fbc-d631630a3878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249288} {:answer/id #uuid "efe3a784-7995-56b3-978c-788102cf3100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249288} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a18d560-640b-5d6d-8616-e9dd57c2577a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7da788f3-581f-57f3-a61d-5c56509defca" :question/math-academy-id "q-114473" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (8+8{\\cot }^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ with $\\theta =4x+3,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡(4x+3)={\\csc }^{2}⁡(4x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (8+8{\\cot }^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x & =8\\int (1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡(4x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)]+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109b22a6-d3dd-5ed0-be45-6c1e67dc6541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247569} {:answer/id #uuid "229a39f8-42eb-5bb6-86d4-bbfff2c4c90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247733} {:answer/id #uuid "506fd08e-5636-5073-8b9b-999fb05b57d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{3}-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248145} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2a25c7-9320-5224-a084-275ada01afa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248516} {:answer/id #uuid "eccb6e3d-85c6-5f1b-b431-8c4a9a157def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247733} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c8a9067-e08c-5e41-a1c1-212dc091969f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fadab91-be05-5653-9838-592471a9067d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114555" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=8{x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 8{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4})-\\int \\frac{2{x}^{4}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c442b7-5812-5b9f-b5a3-9735de7bc5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-2x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247886} {:answer/id #uuid "a66e4f08-82a7-51c7-9f0f-a316a7a82095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-x}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248906} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1079b7-6c59-5146-a689-43939b214e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248935} {:answer/id #uuid "c95f1123-d58f-569d-9f99-fc047c8ff23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+3x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249216} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5f9c6f-c38f-5d5f-aa01-a433d15de579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247886} :answer-field/id #uuid "260a912a-e53c-57b3-a2f5-20d936d8b8f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "805d5bf3-56e5-56a7-8a77-c98eb5fe1df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-114585" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\log }_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int {\\log }_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{2}{2x+1} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\log }_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\int \\frac{2x}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use a substitution $2x+1=u,$ thus $2x=u-1$ and $\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\log }_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\int \\left(\\frac{2x}{2x+1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\int \\frac{u-1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}u+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\frac{u}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{2}+D] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\frac{2x+1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)}{2}+D] \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-2x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=D-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22097ee6-e029-5705-bd0d-12e8438e427a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247726} {:answer/id #uuid "3476300f-6f10-58d8-810a-76e72b26a106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247896} {:answer/id #uuid "46189a0d-05b3-5b4d-b076-027b366be2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248051} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0a2c8c-0d41-5bdb-b6b2-87f65ad145c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248498} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5f4ac2-3246-5235-8484-0b0f64f2b358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7889684-8ec7-5297-81fb-c1d9b326d8d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a5747f9d-1d5b-5a68-8df4-e21d4a5aef56" :question/math-academy-id "q-114597" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/3}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/3}^{e}-{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){|}_{1/3}^{e}-{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}x\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3e)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3e)-x{|}_{1/3}^{e}\\, \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3e)-(e-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+e-e+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f9b9a3-311d-5712-b1f8-0b2cc2693c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248190} {:answer/id #uuid "819c9f6e-eee9-518f-ad4d-dab8c5f22481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248570} {:answer/id #uuid "966730f7-44f6-5c2d-8640-3e5f5ac629da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248761} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fb894f-898b-5b82-a4af-80883361e3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249164} {:answer/id #uuid "dab1de7d-e9e0-50f5-8b17-8824b9b35fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248190} :answer-field/id #uuid "41e34ed1-9695-54d8-96f0-fc2c12813104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "228e02d3-780d-535b-b966-624ae49969cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-114600" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06210c65-cc7a-5e4b-bf69-5482048cc94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247478} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8b6629-4046-5093-b11c-2c9ff8c296aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247704} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4a0cff-9e96-5852-b237-667134b904ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248828} {:answer/id #uuid "ade0ab9f-b410-5ccc-93ed-690c6b0b905b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248978} {:answer/id #uuid "e484aeb9-9230-52f5-85a4-d8f264a2ce27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247478} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcabde0a-e8f2-56da-b3be-7871c9bb28e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "16bb2470-b172-54fa-bb93-68ffa12f7218" :question/math-academy-id "q-114677" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bd605d-4c29-506c-8ce7-c84c48b67fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247627} {:answer/id #uuid "34bd43f9-d4db-533f-acd2-5f7163057588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247897} {:answer/id #uuid "481e05e7-b62d-5851-a787-6a5d10613a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248068} {:answer/id #uuid "8e50b12e-9d6e-5a6e-8673-a420a275d68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248690} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5b9509-017e-5608-ad72-0fc4c95ad848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2a0c2c0-21f1-56dd-ac06-3f6bd57cc988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "91699790-f225-57ae-8ea8-89f90b88a5ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-114679" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\log }_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\log }_{3}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\log }_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[uv{|}_{1/2}^{1}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x){|}_{1/2}^{1}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-x{|}_{1/2}^{1}\\,] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0-(1-\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "415bbc44-7f4c-58ad-8157-8f1f5ed47aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248005} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa4b914-459a-50f8-b61f-f8f5e3451572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248290} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc36f50-7f15-5f26-a2a4-236f583e6504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248521} {:answer/id #uuid "9025b8d7-d7e0-5ff3-b795-bc80c1e96b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248704} {:answer/id #uuid "b3394b3f-3533-568a-a273-e5b8ffd20f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248005} :answer-field/id #uuid "01fbb077-6af9-5c0d-a4c5-a794f5a7f75d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4ba36d0b-ab92-5237-aa17-794cfb598845" :question/math-academy-id "q-114681" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{5x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{2}\\right)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x]}_{0}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot [0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2132f021-21a8-5a4e-af38-2e7201ea6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247718} {:answer/id #uuid "35b40ac4-e4c4-5a30-8877-24568a7820dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247910} {:answer/id #uuid "372e873d-cdee-52c0-a20f-7c350588b44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247922} {:answer/id #uuid "86138e57-324c-5532-8800-362d936df9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248620} {:answer/id #uuid "cf400ea6-3cf8-51e3-b37b-9da66310f2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247718} :answer-field/id #uuid "a610120c-3a38-5275-b0b0-50a70ca22c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "005ce8c5-ca00-5c75-b19b-e74315abd5f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-114685" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{x}^{2}-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c153176-f1be-574a-b61f-f35b724e9466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247532} {:answer/id #uuid "1130fb9f-a5c8-5014-9eac-4952653e89b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247577} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7f6fe8-e41a-559e-b3f5-c1a3707c68d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248395} {:answer/id #uuid "7d64c715-ce63-56cf-b8e4-f014fcf35e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248524} {:answer/id #uuid "9e55b908-564e-5263-ba81-3a007361a635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248395} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2d206e5-c570-5346-9baa-8117910d203b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6789bdc9-30dd-5df6-91b5-90b18efe1bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114688" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({\\sin }^{2}⁡4x-{\\cos }^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x={\\cos }^{2}⁡4x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({\\sin }^{2}⁡4x-{\\cos }^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({\\cos }^{2}⁡4x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049104f3-f0f9-54a7-b34b-435d0972bf29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247457} {:answer/id #uuid "a4eaf12f-fe73-5186-a4a3-9f8f03675c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248897} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ff8b54-32a2-5896-b5ee-9f76878d8456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248926} {:answer/id #uuid "bdae4aa9-7adc-544f-8974-b9d6969f616e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249110} {:answer/id #uuid "beaa505f-9480-5438-ad07-7fb3d61dda35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249118}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249118} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9060f05-b9b2-57a4-af25-27550214ecdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87059ed3-91f0-5463-8df6-00c144965662" :question/math-academy-id "q-114691" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056d0b62-18e9-55f0-8775-8042d2ae824c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(2{x}^{2}+4x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247469} {:answer/id #uuid "4a24ad98-a0b5-5cc3-a600-8812772a324b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-2x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248085} {:answer/id #uuid "75795d17-a5ee-582f-9b9e-c0ff0d8637fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(2{x}^{2}-2x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248456} {:answer/id #uuid "ab67a9b5-9a63-5859-b7bb-9c8da59f41ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248948} {:answer/id #uuid "beb86753-1823-5658-bf1a-e594d3c2bfcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}({x}^{2}-2x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248948} :answer-field/id #uuid "8047459c-94d3-55f6-8eca-48002be93179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1074dd87-580f-51cd-9b83-51cf6a648213" :question/math-academy-id "q-114693" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{2x}(2x-1)-4D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f4a15a-5034-5e4e-a608-b3f646db186e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{3x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247511} {:answer/id #uuid "16c0d414-1f11-53d0-852d-fc7267102858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247628} {:answer/id #uuid "62455da9-48ef-550d-a440-01a2ee5979f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248309} {:answer/id #uuid "853821a9-0766-5644-bceb-497ae5f69fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248610} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a57500-2707-5bb0-be03-9a6fe0084671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247511} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aeb11c3-2186-54f3-989f-bc6bf929857f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c6cefcd4-0ca3-54cc-a93f-201d1a8af19f" :question/math-academy-id "q-114696" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4{\\cos }^{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\theta =x$ and substituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{\\cos }^{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x)}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({\\cos }^{2}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the same formula $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1$ with $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({\\cos }^{2}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\frac{3}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+\\frac{3}{2}\\,x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{3x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc23f83-d209-56fe-ba9f-4d4ea28149d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(-4{x}^{2}+2x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247528} {:answer/id #uuid "347405c1-4237-56c7-8c18-11e44217feec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(-8{x}^{2}+4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247895} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7e723e-dbfd-5048-8cdb-9b45ca1d33f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{8}(-4{x}^{2}+2x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247987} {:answer/id #uuid "d0824483-e541-59f6-a789-31210d1c98a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249273} {:answer/id #uuid "e68c2af6-fc8e-55c4-a7ed-0bca24c425da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{8}(2{x}^{2}+x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247895} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b20a23-0102-5d8b-a89a-d0ed94ee9a91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c03c84f2-a54b-5cd7-81f9-a3bec98efab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114699" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=1-2{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)(-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(-8{x}^{2}+4x+3)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79350746-f178-547e-ba32-a73629e0bdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248487} {:answer/id #uuid "80fdd38c-10cb-5e31-9bdf-05a2c0ede7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248562} {:answer/id #uuid "a59d4ba7-54bc-5e85-8f04-df9ccb943007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248902} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0a71fe-aa09-5164-a615-0169aaa1adc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249239} {:answer/id #uuid "faefdfc4-dfa5-5604-b2ff-09b6d5b22666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248902} :answer-field/id #uuid "100ab088-a79d-58b2-9f15-678f690ca31e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7bceb2aa-4302-5440-9a58-9fffc3e95c17" :question/math-academy-id "q-114703" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({\\sin }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({\\sin }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1e6aba-be6e-5ad9-a570-9f5b6c5579a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247542} {:answer/id #uuid "24a1ac18-1b92-561d-9f2f-5b8340427947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247747} {:answer/id #uuid "60c4dea3-3499-52e9-be7b-e62e150e5240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248302} {:answer/id #uuid "80c59204-a318-55d0-882f-4c0026b93bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248557} {:answer/id #uuid "de29d0d8-e2ee-58e7-b612-b8e345c7aaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249393}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249393} :answer-field/id #uuid "95575fc0-fd7c-5a07-bb21-caf4767c4581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "18d4b96f-8021-5acc-99b3-397a7e245847" :question/math-academy-id "q-114706" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\cdot 2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x-2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+6(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+3{x}^{2}+C \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56b4881f-232b-5196-af35-aea7cb58e62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248212} {:answer/id #uuid "5968f717-2e1f-500b-b4d7-ccdd473f65be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248233} {:answer/id #uuid "60480242-e3f6-5bd4-97c9-e03d8a634da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248296} {:answer/id #uuid "75629651-ad7e-5b79-a799-0589d593b3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248455} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa029d1-b400-582d-911d-327f96487132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249406}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248296} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b0fcafe-3643-59b7-8506-b7d096e4d110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "638cdd8d-a193-51d5-8d07-1c091b8aea29" :question/math-academy-id "q-114709" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int [1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8e6af2-4d5c-5c2a-a223-b1581d70cf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247539} {:answer/id #uuid "81d0e0bb-c0c1-5d45-a394-2bbf8970a683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248575} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8ed56a-5c7f-5ef2-81bb-dd4b2068da0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248685} {:answer/id #uuid "b663df30-c9cb-50e7-be06-34aabcf17e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249056} {:answer/id #uuid "ec862b5c-e91d-5ccc-9ab8-a560591305a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249501} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fd4bc8-95db-5454-a58a-0b3490f1540b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f620a8f2-ded3-5ab5-a180-a9e0b77ecbff" :question/math-academy-id "q-114712" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d328673-8a58-50a7-adf0-77833bda83a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248393} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6b7145-93de-5653-8e93-690166d427e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248974} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b1913f-f2b9-558c-9e85-1c381520273a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249323} {:answer/id #uuid "dff13be0-607d-5cb2-80a6-212887c63715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249407} {:answer/id #uuid "e13421bd-fa04-57e8-b8b6-66240918236e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249417}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249323} :answer-field/id #uuid "48fe523b-b6b3-53f5-bcc1-804af608577e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f255ed2-421f-593a-aea8-7435217040f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-114879" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrr} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & 3 & 1 \\\\ \\hline  & 3 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}=3x+1+\\frac{2}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3x+1+\\frac{2}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e38e5f-e743-5255-b946-3f87403bb592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247748} {:answer/id #uuid "5c546415-3863-5d3f-b10c-1b5acd9dc9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248263} {:answer/id #uuid "86aaead3-1c5a-5904-ac13-8ed5b6145a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248626} {:answer/id #uuid "aad103a1-3853-5320-abe6-b1c36055c766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248944} {:answer/id #uuid "fb71bd34-6591-5202-b50d-715b19bae866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247748} :answer-field/id #uuid "f639f4f8-bc4c-5c49-9600-af210e2a046e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "420c0e34-183e-5e05-8101-22873fd779c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-11488" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=3{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/4e/4e3758ddf652c3dd7df10d381b42ca1dde50d48250bed672b740329191202597.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/54/54ad3b4c8bc3aef707af764cd8d8badf9390737dc75491aa66ea554f531a7576.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(3{x}^{2})-(4{x}^{2}-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2((1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f54fcd2-11eb-5de2-b3f7-146de7b6348a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}+2{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247564} {:answer/id #uuid "407f3c19-f6db-5e87-b664-92774974ae3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x({\\log }_{4}⁡(x)+\\frac{2}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247993} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0cac64-7a75-5a03-b08a-cee4bcbfa551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x({({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}+\\frac{{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248695} {:answer/id #uuid "d24d95fe-f787-520e-9b19-d413da18936f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x({\\log }_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249291} {:answer/id #uuid "e86cde0d-e490-5e48-8a63-d7e0865c34b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x({({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249477} :answer-field/id #uuid "e420d51c-8836-5780-9cc3-1975930de065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "40de2008-379c-588d-bf38-59107f150b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-114880" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({\\log }_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({\\log }_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{\\log }_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({\\log }_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2{\\log }_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({\\log }_{4}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({\\log }_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(x{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =x{({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =x({({\\log }_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18bbba15-3ca2-548d-aa0b-fa8fde96be41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\frac{13}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247648} {:answer/id #uuid "505545e5-5b59-5235-b0a9-6bfde088dc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248143} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c7d6f-7d36-5780-986c-93149710dad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{17}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248533} {:answer/id #uuid "7e81ded9-2f3b-592a-8788-962d13bc36f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{11}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248537} {:answer/id #uuid "abfdae4f-4dff-5106-9d49-8bae29e9c2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\frac{17}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248954} :answer-field/id #uuid "a24575db-05cf-5008-adac-d417701d5102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "759dad06-62ad-59d4-9d65-70a46d8005a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-114892" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2(x+\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 3 & -4 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\hline  & 3 & -\\frac{17}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-4}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})} & =3-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{17}{2(x+\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{17}{2x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3-\\frac{17}{2x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[3{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x-17{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[3x-\\frac{17}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[3(2-0)-\\frac{17}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2(2)+3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2(0)+3|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[6-\\frac{17}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[6-\\frac{17}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =3-\\frac{17}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d2a00f-4a5e-50ec-95a7-3d34717ca878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[3-11\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247508} {:answer/id #uuid "4193cca1-11ab-5816-befe-e6e7082fd2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}[1-17\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248007} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff3945d-cc27-5c31-8969-8fc40195197f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}[4-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248409} {:answer/id #uuid "94b6c9b3-1db7-55a8-8ada-45bd1cf3adf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}[3-17\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248746} {:answer/id #uuid "ca649297-0c30-5fd0-a460-252be27d14b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}[17-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248746} :answer-field/id #uuid "e407e99d-a50c-5c35-b3e1-dbdcca77665b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cf68c363-607b-5ed8-bb76-a69145831fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-114894" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3(x-\\frac{1}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{(x-\\frac{1}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 2 & 5 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\hline  & 2 & \\frac{17}{3}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+5}{(x-\\frac{1}{3})} & =2+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{17}{3}\\right)}{(x-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{17}{3(x-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{17}{3x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2+\\frac{17}{3x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\text{d}x+17{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{1}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[2x+\\frac{17}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2(0-(-1))+\\frac{17}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3(0)-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3(-1)-1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2(1)+\\frac{17}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2-\\frac{17}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2-\\frac{34}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[3-17\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9826cc-221a-58f0-a949-003259d10830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}(1+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247516} {:answer/id #uuid "1935d4e4-d30d-5335-ab50-3b3fa04366e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}(1+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247651} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9aeadf-fded-5a2b-861d-58af4c73c465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}(5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247959} {:answer/id #uuid "54ac800d-c455-530d-b963-3aea2be7c145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}(3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248185} {:answer/id #uuid "6b902fe9-3b07-57e2-870c-2a793e643e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}(1+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248379} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3226bde-c895-54e8-98cf-b5b2df79f6fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "462b058c-0216-5b9a-aa39-6bb6d7bc768e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114896" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $6$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6(x+\\frac{1}{6})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & 2 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\hline  & 2 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})} & =2+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{4}{6x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}(2+\\frac{4}{6x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[2x+\\frac{4}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6x+1|]}_{-1/12}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[2x+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6x+1|]}_{-1/12}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2(0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{12}\\right))+\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6(0)+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6\\left(-\\frac{1}{12}\\right)+1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)+\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}(1+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b73f700-d96a-599b-ab52-2db5d3386e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247811} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfc4874-a112-5f03-a9d1-39829d4222c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[2-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247956} {:answer/id #uuid "4924bf57-6609-582a-b504-14c54c2c2e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248075} {:answer/id #uuid "55342b69-6990-55a7-b6b9-79464a3e57e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248192} {:answer/id #uuid "9991218e-5d69-54aa-88aa-8ca966e8ab5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248075} :answer-field/id #uuid "4388d5da-7f71-581a-8f21-f145c319a5de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e67ca108-b4a2-50be-81c1-fe32c84586b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114898" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $4$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4(x-\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 8 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & 2 \\\\ \\hline  & 8 & 3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x+1}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})} & =8+\\frac{3}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =8+\\frac{12}{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}(8+\\frac{12}{4x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\text{d}x+12{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-1|]_{-1/8}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8(0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right))+3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4(0)-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)+3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082f5ffa-db8f-5b86-9f1f-66bec8888277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247495} {:answer/id #uuid "312a7284-86a2-58d1-925a-aae469b9cc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+2}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247869} {:answer/id #uuid "4646f294-569b-5c9a-8e3f-8b4bffc32dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2e}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248052} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ab61f-f876-54ff-ac9a-719572398e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248654} {:answer/id #uuid "aed8ce21-2fef-51ea-932a-bf3d76234eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248654} :answer-field/id #uuid "90297971-15bc-5633-a065-ce4100255f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dca36867-5a6f-51bd-9e64-ad043dde12a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114900" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({\\log }_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{2{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{\\log }_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({\\log }_{3}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(2{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${\\log }_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({\\log }_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(2{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(2{\\ln }^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(2{\\ln }^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c50259-96ff-5091-b6d8-d010aabcd87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2}-3}{4{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247435} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1b58be-7936-591f-a3c9-e22825f21d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248249} {:answer/id #uuid "602d95a8-3c49-5628-a5fc-26ae6d4442af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248293} {:answer/id #uuid "ae45cdbd-b5db-5833-8170-fccfe8733b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248981} {:answer/id #uuid "f16fc0ad-7654-55ab-873b-8984cf712d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248981} :answer-field/id #uuid "46bceeef-1e66-56f1-87c4-0b40ebcc4c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7237523-38ec-5167-93d0-30747905ffb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114942" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({\\log }_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({\\log }_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({\\log }_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({\\log }_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(3{x}^{3})-\\int (3{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({\\log }_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({\\log }_{2}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({\\log }_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{3}{({\\log }_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({\\log }_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}{\\log }_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}(3{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{6D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${\\log }_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({\\log }_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}(3{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}(3{\\ln }^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}(3{\\ln }^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}(3-2+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}(0-0+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}}{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f84bc89-0f5f-5174-b311-6b1ac207d432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4e-8}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248845} {:answer/id #uuid "acd12eca-de9b-50f1-ae82-10635e26c184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-6}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248968} {:answer/id #uuid "f04115a5-6b2e-5bc2-9ed5-4e7dbb828a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249532} {:answer/id #uuid "f2594c73-abaa-58dd-995b-af12abd8461c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249549} {:answer/id #uuid "f538ac92-7bb7-5776-a593-eda1db9ee897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4e+3}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248845} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b1851a2-09ca-5e98-9283-451c4f3c0948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ee94ab2f-1f66-5795-8930-e1ab98c8dab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114944" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{4{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =x{({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4x{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{\\ln }^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}({\\ln }^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${\\log }_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{({\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}({\\ln }^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}({\\ln }^{2}⁡({e}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})+8)-\\frac{1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}({\\ln }^{2}⁡({1}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(4-8+8)-\\frac{1}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}(0-0+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-8}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101c128a-6dc8-5632-a7ae-f0b91b164739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{5}+3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247565} {:answer/id #uuid "2adceece-a53a-577c-9b55-b2e47ed35eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{5}-2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247806} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5aca5e-dc8e-5ba7-9af6-c2a386609510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{5}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247817} {:answer/id #uuid "a227ec20-ef2b-5e2e-b4e4-2aca175b50f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17{e}^{5}-2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248872} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbef6d4-187f-5f47-a784-698d678b5e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{e}^{5}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248872} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3c2387-20aa-5008-8cd7-6fc006779faf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d87d568c-f1e1-5dbc-b0f7-7053ed2197d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114946" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=25{x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 25{x}^{4}\\text{d}x=5{x}^{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(5{x}^{5})-\\int (5{x}^{5})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10(\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D) \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}-10D \\\\  & ={x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-10D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{5})]-[{1}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{5})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5-2+\\frac{2}{5})]-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{17{e}^{5}-2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018d8348-3190-5eea-b417-c63541e4aff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247430} {:answer/id #uuid "08eab5fa-3385-5df5-b8fd-2f29432c498e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247499} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb0449b-6ed5-5ab7-9e17-f2f9074c0612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247696} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1524c0-3b53-58d5-b338-ecb0ead5ac3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247832} {:answer/id #uuid "67f62f68-93fb-55cb-a9ad-f01fff119dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247430} :answer-field/id #uuid "078894b3-54fc-5c41-9c56-f018aef4c9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9725f40a-ccc5-5b84-9193-a09e8482b8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115218" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x+1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ (x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ (x+1)(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le -\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge \\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302b378f-be12-5ba0-97fd-06dd7fe61909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\le -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247858} {:answer/id #uuid "3173ff57-51bd-5dc4-8c31-633792b6a1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247872} {:answer/id #uuid "433231b9-72f8-5a2a-b270-d72099dbaf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248027} {:answer/id #uuid "cb82884c-5f42-579d-a3b2-766dcd5e9b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249235} {:answer/id #uuid "f6af4d9b-c12c-51d1-a663-b88288cd8554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247858} :answer-field/id #uuid "759dac82-e131-5166-b8ec-519724d4e077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac4b46ad-428b-596b-a6dd-aa26aa057694" :question/math-academy-id "q-115222" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{x+6}+1\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{x+6}+1 & \\ge 2 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{x+6} & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{x+6}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ (x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+6)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+6)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6) & \\le \\frac{0}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x+6 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le -6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e749b95-3859-5caf-bfd8-c834793e8ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x<5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247694} {:answer/id #uuid "40bebdef-ae8a-5d9e-ac28-6228e738db2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247995} {:answer/id #uuid "49f23bbb-2f76-5966-bbae-3c9d22acc9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248082} {:answer/id #uuid "b99ff983-b6ef-5e58-8aa0-af927de0b3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x>2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249083} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0c71b-a6be-59ae-b32b-dde21ce862fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x<2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248082} :answer-field/id #uuid "7730106b-6087-572a-9b32-b26564608b47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c08f0a1-4fee-59ab-b31f-f6a9816dc7a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115250" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${5}^{x-1}<{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-1} & <{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2-x}] \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e785456-0d46-526f-9e67-18d57eadc06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247557} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9b3b46-47ed-564d-81ee-53957c02a43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247803} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbce6e1-d015-5841-ac53-b3bc03022688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248277} {:answer/id #uuid "cecf1feb-5477-5f51-924d-678fd3092009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{4}{x-2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249257} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bba209-bdae-5fcc-b9cd-f07175cf1210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{4}{x-2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248277} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a5dba1e-963f-540d-840f-945c1bcdcb43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "804e099a-7eac-522b-b475-41496ab8d992" :question/math-academy-id "q-115323" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4x-10=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)-10 & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)-10 & =A(0-2)+B \\\\ -10 & =-2A-2 \\\\ -2A & =-8 \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{(x-2)}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{(x-2)}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\right)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b51c530-c2cd-5423-a3a0-a04f58d1ce0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247949} {:answer/id #uuid "982a28ab-3d7b-5e19-b85a-f431290e5fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248781} {:answer/id #uuid "afdc1155-89aa-5b60-97c6-f8fa61a52991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-8,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249003} {:answer/id #uuid "ca478ceb-a3a2-5f3d-aa94-ce07df53b76d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249225} {:answer/id #uuid "fef72665-29dd-5651-80da-727cf6075903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248781} :answer-field/id #uuid "064d3cd7-1ed9-5bcb-aee9-f4d6fa025653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "032b9fca-ff0f-5251-a672-9ed3b0e3a341" :question/math-academy-id "q-115346" :question/problem "![](images/84/84a9754d9291ba38d6cd257056d20896ce2ff89b8ee98210c8789fc9d633ae5f.png)\n\nFind the coordinates $(h,k)$ of the center of the circle above given that the points $(-1,4)$ and $(-7,-4)$ lie on a diameter of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. Therefore, we can calculate it using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-1-7}{2},\\frac{4-4}{2}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{8}{2},\\frac{0}{2}) \\\\  & =(-4,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104a37e8-f6cc-5ec9-a48f-51d64001ade6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247566} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3b391a-eaaf-5545-921e-ba51e2369aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247957} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7c2c19-862f-595f-8097-e427b65885c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248403} {:answer/id #uuid "eed826b1-26bf-5b30-8634-29ffd3b2cf85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249520} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc8360c-bc00-5182-82d5-a3b8baeeed0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247957} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f44352e-bdd6-5788-932e-ae381b870850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3a17cc8d-0938-5ad1-ae94-e242754caed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115353" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3}{(3x-2)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{(3x-2)^{5}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{u}^{-4})|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{256}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1be917-67fb-578e-954b-abd43a23e348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247974} {:answer/id #uuid "50cbb7df-ff1d-5ec1-b975-423eb45cf6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248148} {:answer/id #uuid "5a30d855-606b-58c6-b70c-741050c6c8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248240} {:answer/id #uuid "c1157ca6-9bce-51d1-a9ee-ceec63429c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249140} {:answer/id #uuid "ce75318e-e092-5621-a3d1-bcdfb26a886b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248240} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8ddb9e-3675-514c-a828-85dac3fe037b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ef69b6da-6b79-5e57-a05c-34b900661ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115517" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[x]^{′}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "350e964f-b0c5-5cc8-aa3e-689138b63395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247899} {:answer/id #uuid "3d70272f-c760-5b44-9931-6ff944bb069b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247966} {:answer/id #uuid "682d8226-3695-5244-b7c3-bc637b285c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248359} {:answer/id #uuid "80f6e4ff-228c-5008-914a-73923accb57a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248561} {:answer/id #uuid "e8068369-ef65-53dc-94bc-885b350faeaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248561} :answer-field/id #uuid "4512e4e7-bf35-5b55-bd12-e0de031dd2a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4d575ad-474a-547c-88ad-41cb9bf33eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115519" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x)=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(x+1)=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x}{x+1} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x]^{′}}{{[x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{1-x}-2)}{1} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{1-x}+2) \\\\  & =-(0+2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01229e17-29dc-5c11-a1d0-77f98e694104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247422} {:answer/id #uuid "01764684-3b80-58ae-8acb-d199ca383b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{4}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247427} {:answer/id #uuid "6472ae3c-1932-599e-8442-efa43b1a187c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{3}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248332} {:answer/id #uuid "75dd0b93-cc35-536a-bc7e-41f0639d6d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{1}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248462} {:answer/id #uuid "8f983273-e025-5683-9a9e-4b9ba07daf6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{1}{x-2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247422} :answer-field/id #uuid "18897162-969d-5851-8bc8-7ca7734669de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a2506c51-af3a-5dc3-a5a7-44e29ebb4d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-115537" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{x(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{x(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-2}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-12x+4=A(x-2)^{2}+Bx(x-2)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)^{2}-12(2)+4 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2)(2-2)+C(2) \\\\ -4 & =2C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}-12(0)+4 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0)(0-2)+C(0) \\\\ 4 & =4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{2}-12(1)+4 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1)(1-2)+C(1) \\\\ -4 & =A-B+C \\\\ -4 & =1-B+(-2) \\\\ -3 & =-B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6ccd60-a3e4-5ce6-9d5e-a376f267d343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248406} {:answer/id #uuid "71f7119c-74ea-52d7-a308-7659883775e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248418} {:answer/id #uuid "84779d56-58c2-555d-a3cc-90c133ddaec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} {:answer/id #uuid "b961e3af-c091-5872-9218-c014fd5deaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249081} {:answer/id #uuid "fc333b6b-03c6-59c2-97cd-44b7a727e392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} :answer-field/id #uuid "8264505c-0613-5f02-b2e2-463f0471a6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4bc1f703-1de2-59b6-981e-831238ea97de" :question/math-academy-id "q-115592" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & ≈L\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043d1ce5-9e3e-5519-9a4c-03569c147664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247455} {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} {:answer/id #uuid "64b65e65-4617-5e48-b1cf-a0372e6395b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{3}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248333} {:answer/id #uuid "7379ce95-99ed-5b20-9302-b338646b620c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248436} {:answer/id #uuid "d091e770-03db-512b-a3b7-40a0f2053ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aca0b8a-ad9d-5b96-a5a6-ae937ea506b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "513d3507-74c5-5ea9-ac09-4c99e11dedbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115623" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}=A(2x+1)^{2}+B(2x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =A{(2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1)}^{2}+B(2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2} & =A(2(0)+1)^{2}+B(2(0)+1)+C \\\\ 0 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)^{2} & =A(2(-1)+1)^{2}+B(2(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=3$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e99392-3fb8-593f-bb71-927a2245ef60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|-\\frac{1}{x-6}+\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247475} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec9dd0d-b640-5c43-a725-0042e481b946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|-\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248283} {:answer/id #uuid "86b5dda6-2a86-54fb-bede-1e0417d886be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{2}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248629} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecb478a-3007-5d46-953a-fe88b0e8b34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{2}{x-6}+\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248692} {:answer/id #uuid "9c599937-5df4-540c-be04-7d31cfb05087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248815} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8a9229-5a1a-5fbe-8ee7-9df606b90cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "17b68d5d-09c0-504c-99d5-2bccfb24d4f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115655" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-6}+\\frac{B}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-6)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-6)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+9x-16=A(x-6)^{2}+B(x-6)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(6)^{2}+9(6)-16 & =A(6-6)^{2}+B(6-6)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=5$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(5)^{2}+9(5)-16 & =A(5-6)^{2}+B(5-6)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+2 \\\\ A-B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=7$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(7)^{2}+9(7)-16 & =A(7-6)^{2}+B(7-6)+C \\\\ -2 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=2$ and $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =2-4 \\\\ A & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+B & =-4 \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-5)^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423478f8-9aa2-5cda-afb3-17b256545aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015} {:answer/id #uuid "4aee625f-94b6-586f-a54a-d0e72230fad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3x+1}-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248094} {:answer/id #uuid "874ccc30-a28e-56ec-9671-fcc5053cb332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{4}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248634} {:answer/id #uuid "98869dd8-a1e3-512a-8c1e-3372b8c5a8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248785} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a83092-cd3f-5932-8afb-77fe5a6631f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{1}{3x+1}+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cafb8c1-951c-5e34-ba7e-1900668fa6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "031d8c13-fb73-52e6-afed-79556c97e9a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115656" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{3x+1}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+1=A(3x+1)^{2}+B(3x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+1 & =A{(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1)}^{2}+B(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)^{2}+1 & =A(3(0)+1)^{2}+B(3(0)+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(-1)^{2}+1 & =A(3(-1)+1)^{2}+B(3(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A-2B+2 \\\\ 4A-2B & =8 \\\\ 2A-B & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $2A-B=4$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =4-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d510ad-e4c6-5b08-97e2-145c27b946b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x+8}+\\frac{2}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247487} {:answer/id #uuid "359169cf-7dfa-5fd2-b41b-65dfb064eccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|-\\frac{2}{x+8}+\\frac{3}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247908} {:answer/id #uuid "6b603583-9558-5345-9f72-ac375dd8cd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x+8}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248378} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee712ae-afa4-548d-a83e-6f57e098e203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248837} {:answer/id #uuid "f310db86-e9eb-5f4c-9d1c-94714b2a79a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249552} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c3b99d3-685d-5960-9262-fdb7cc5ba128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6058468b-fca6-572c-b924-db85b67482b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115662" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+8}+\\frac{B}{(x+8)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+8)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+8)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-2=A(x+8)^{2}+B(x+8)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-8,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)-2 & =A(-8+8)^{2}+B(-8+8)+C \\\\ -10 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=-7$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)-2 & =A(-7+8)^{2}+B(-7+8)+C \\\\ -9 & =A+B-10 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-9$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)-2 & =A(-9+8)^{2}+B(-9+8)+C \\\\ -11 & =A-B-10 \\\\ A-B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-1$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-1+1 \\\\ 2A & =0 \\\\ A & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+B & =1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-10\\int \\frac{1}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54d923c2-b2f8-5d1c-a1fc-68a056f22c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248187} {:answer/id #uuid "990bfcfa-1f99-553c-a8ba-07aa7350fb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248791} {:answer/id #uuid "b521e98f-6c0a-5d30-b8c2-17d7c4a8510a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249044} {:answer/id #uuid "cf02c307-1d50-5567-ac85-0f7d14bc9b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249261} {:answer/id #uuid "dece391e-5beb-53bd-9d1e-fa41c6cd1e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249400}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249044} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8347731-1849-5e44-ab0d-b77e59a7eb41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b1b65b0-769f-5dc5-a09c-d74cd95adc54" :question/math-academy-id "q-115665" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+5(0)-2 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+5) \\\\ -2 & =B-5 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+3}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+5=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d86c2f6-a314-54ab-8dcf-d3faec6a6acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247547} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf83c5f-777a-5606-b25b-ad1a2d27450f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248387} {:answer/id #uuid "884fc272-292f-5726-b2b3-9221568754c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248642} {:answer/id #uuid "bad95a73-618f-506a-a148-60bbb75b007b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249092} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d383ca-dc7c-5b9d-8c65-750c36a50197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249579} :answer-field/id #uuid "348ab176-46a8-53a5-a44b-b51495ebb88c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fcac2c1-8de9-5c0d-89db-ccf7066e1090" :question/math-academy-id "q-115667" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+20 & =(x-2)(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+20 & =(0-2)(0+2)+D({0}^{2}+8) \\\\ 20 & =-4+8D \\\\ 24 & =8D \\\\ D & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+8=u.$ Then, $x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf1b20f-3f1e-5d39-b833-924bd37ace5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247541} {:answer/id #uuid "1778ff91-6f16-5705-8283-db705faace3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247636} {:answer/id #uuid "30031e73-eda6-5628-a936-a4c56fda636b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247857} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0be9ab-d229-5515-9f4f-676bfd0e8f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249222} {:answer/id #uuid "e64b227d-d62e-52ef-8489-7acf21e9c49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247636} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f2bda8-8928-56d1-a14f-bbe51de1a1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa383850-ed95-5745-b056-448c57db2176" :question/math-academy-id "q-115673" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-27 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-5(0)-27 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+20) \\\\ -27 & =B-20 \\\\ B & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-7}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+20=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d004b1-8a79-5237-9a43-c5d695947628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247474} {:answer/id #uuid "73d79229-ca1a-597f-bc97-2007a1959580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248441} {:answer/id #uuid "859ad402-4287-5864-9a3a-b27433df0b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248615} {:answer/id #uuid "d816ae40-b699-5487-b9be-edd17a53da31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249333} {:answer/id #uuid "fbddd6e4-1678-53af-9fac-a34da478aee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248615} :answer-field/id #uuid "39da731f-4eac-5eb0-8967-592e81989b3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "54337009-4b4f-594c-8090-7c81fe847987" :question/math-academy-id "q-115686" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-9x+5 & =(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047c28c1-7366-514f-8a71-70b8574e8b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247456} {:answer/id #uuid "10cab1b6-0eef-57ac-9c3c-e79de55b6805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247573} {:answer/id #uuid "567e1f65-c305-5e5b-8580-4126bb121c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248209} {:answer/id #uuid "aef9856b-bea1-549a-9bcd-ac2c1039a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248991} {:answer/id #uuid "d72b33b4-9d9a-5a6a-bd0d-01f35633bd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249329} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d57d04-325f-59f4-bb1d-89f0fcd9bb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "412d22df-d958-56dd-8884-22116d9638ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-115690" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+6$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+6}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+6)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x & =(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-4,$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+6| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06faf40-267a-585a-a698-8d1c4a8418b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248857} {:answer/id #uuid "b11b9a13-576c-58f2-b1fa-068e66f1591c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249010} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c1daa1-28ba-5945-bf62-2d01df1e7ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249162} {:answer/id #uuid "e31429d4-0443-599a-a3a4-365a3f4492c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249430} {:answer/id #uuid "ebec7c76-3253-5217-8050-0e4ee5b66e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249496} :answer-field/id #uuid "4adf0cfe-17c4-56d0-b154-4a4709b2b3f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b36c8e07-4439-57ae-b9d1-276ef485c6ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-115693" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+3$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+5x-9 & =(Ax+B)(x+4)+C({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e0f5cbb-98e8-55aa-b890-984b305ed4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248530} {:answer/id #uuid "94947933-b100-53c7-8fdb-0b6d8803da1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248745} {:answer/id #uuid "95219ef7-6026-599f-b799-3fe1f3c2a17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248749} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9d1e02-75e2-51b5-87f3-47ea9e6004dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249502} {:answer/id #uuid "f80a858d-c201-59de-aa39-33d3a1e7d443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249502} :answer-field/id #uuid "d433bef3-6036-5aac-9df3-3dc1353dab2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "74feace7-d730-58bc-b03e-4652e0e5c170" :question/math-academy-id "q-115710" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(4)^{2}-4(1)(-6)=40\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{40}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}+4x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+12x-12=A({x}^{2}+4x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=2$, and $C=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x+4)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ddb4ed-90f0-5df5-b21a-41d53d8fe661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}{3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248021} {:answer/id #uuid "49d41c54-3f94-52cd-a66b-4ba669efe7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248079} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f4069d-2ea6-59d1-acc4-c6d742ba2c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249043} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7d86ae-66d8-5482-ac29-aa13eed6597e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249254} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c6e3ba-2427-5970-9188-62ee0b23275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249284} :answer-field/id #uuid "6905e022-77c6-597f-b4cb-140b5c642224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d0e590b-4ba3-5c1f-9a3a-31d9c817ed33" :question/math-academy-id "q-115712" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(5)^{2}-4(2)(-6)=73\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{73}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n12-5x=A(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-2,B=4$, and $C=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}+5x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88fc7cc8-79d5-59d3-ad2c-04b64b84f0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461755} {:answer/id #uuid "6262e720-b181-540c-bdb0-37bb9b9572a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461755} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f91efd-4f5f-59b4-b78b-62d93d7a34fd" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462631} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8590a88d-c93e-5cb6-834a-cd06d60f91f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461757} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2dccd5-90a7-5939-a2f3-ba68c00652df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461758} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d29f83-bdb4-5fcd-8a8c-93e6a7035123" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80541672-0915-5b6c-9017-9ac341bd9f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461759} {:answer/id #uuid "a2defdda-9879-5425-b8f7-043e2815e940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461759} :answer-field/id #uuid "77bf2e3a-fe25-597e-9fd5-53baa9f801cd" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "988f2d4b-1d26-5c62-baed-2061d6f81ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115751" :question/problem "![](images/25/2552222c6a0be1b1bf1e67a66b4906bb735c2d974d724d3e25e988d15388c696.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/eb/eb1303132a4fe6a26c85b4733a3039a52109b4c49408e1c82038d71885d27f70.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1,$ but it also approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{8}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=-\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0181da5-8614-58af-ac14-1a9336d4dbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461761} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2eec4c-4f47-514b-bf5d-f2c38895ac0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461761} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc6800d-212e-50df-85f3-d5b35178961d" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=7-{x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84b32df7-b0d9-5ac9-b15a-2fa4213ca142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461763} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4b5a3f-874e-5bd2-a618-5e76d2280ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461763} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bef5e3b-3082-5dd7-8c06-9ea6d0a08d4c" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462635} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0de8a2f-f221-5129-bf2e-e14033c84895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461765} {:answer/id #uuid "61d491ae-9078-571e-8681-ef4d10f41b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461766} :answer-field/id #uuid "61918825-a1f6-5a04-9d13-9a6f5063d1ec" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3be4e44a-f534-5d30-81ef-fc22fbf18076" :question/math-academy-id "q-115754" :question/problem "![](images/34/34af8d1078100aea331c300ea529d2fdefbdd31e99ee8a7946325d58a8abf680.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/d0/d02883b776dbcd55c5c96347ed1a7bb0e51ef8c26e62786049b6585b878d04fb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-7.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=7-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -7.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1+\\frac{7}{x})}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{x+7}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2e5bd3-f473-510b-9434-aa7443cb05b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247670} {:answer/id #uuid "1d717368-1df9-5a63-8977-b45159997003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247687} {:answer/id #uuid "89616672-693d-59fb-88ad-7b1432546374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248644} {:answer/id #uuid "a9969d54-7454-5477-b1ca-0b4165edbcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248931} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ccf359-d194-5c0f-99a1-734608e48ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247687} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d962fb6-9b43-5d0e-8fc2-499dbcc92713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "734835be-6e59-529d-977d-cd321f0badd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115772" :question/problem "Given that $r=\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27edf7e2-1949-5b19-8951-ef3c63f01da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247781} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea79f41-fe25-516b-bca1-bc3026a8967b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247839} {:answer/id #uuid "8417dd2f-c817-521f-8b3a-8a613dc15c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248592} {:answer/id #uuid "a49939f7-f7ec-5933-b7fb-eda0850f82be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248891} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd0cdbd-93c2-547d-a725-3c921538ee20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248592} :answer-field/id #uuid "df247728-6fc4-5d6e-ad05-31984fda9cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b468347a-5af4-5e0d-add1-8cb76b34a17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115776" :question/problem "Given that $r=-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{16} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aacad592-c837-5a68-8f8b-57c3d7dcb43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248943} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d09c12-1f86-5e78-8465-e8c7d48b9e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249017} {:answer/id #uuid "b2637471-8d8a-5bbe-bd12-5b87dfc106b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249023} {:answer/id #uuid "d0726f1e-da5c-51b1-9a97-44846fb7928e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249271} {:answer/id #uuid "f632b040-af98-517b-9654-3f33648e9c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249023} :answer-field/id #uuid "02e92776-40a0-56fe-8e7e-05efd3ee58e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b056bc9-1b86-5285-9289-f5246eedec51" :question/math-academy-id "q-115788" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}=\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $m=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by ${m}^{3},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}) & ={m}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}\\right) \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+{m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & ={m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ \\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}}+\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} & =\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} \\\\ 3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m+1 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m-2m & =3-1 \\\\ 2m & =2 \\\\ m & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=1\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b0fb4a6-a0a8-5f73-8a19-abd9ab8d7b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247947} {:answer/id #uuid "512c886f-c605-5743-8740-b7c0b7952acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248153} {:answer/id #uuid "a792d170-bee0-5203-810d-3149d20c6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248914} {:answer/id #uuid "b68ef072-78e4-53b5-8670-2baeaf867b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249060} {:answer/id #uuid "b69e7dcd-1896-5e5e-b67d-4d01a47dee7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248914} :answer-field/id #uuid "87bed9b6-d71c-5c36-af6d-4aec4c0b6168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb3909a1-5d95-5bcb-832e-e8e09e292f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-115791" :question/problem "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{z+1}{2z}=2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $z=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ 2z\\cdot \\left(\\frac{z+1}{2z}\\right) & =2z\\cdot (2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}) \\\\ 2z\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2z\\cdot 2-2z\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ \\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} & =2z\\cdot 2-\\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ z+1 & =4z-(2z+1) \\\\ z+1 & =4z-2z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =4z-2z-1 \\\\ z+1 & =2z-1 \\\\ -z & =-2 \\\\ z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db9a03b-7189-51c6-beb4-58184628399a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247551} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffff95f-e0da-51b7-b2b1-60ad819c5e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247856} {:answer/id #uuid "5a100c5a-10c6-507e-ab3d-717c80187730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248239} {:answer/id #uuid "de207cc8-0ebf-5357-9d93-bba635b2e97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249392} {:answer/id #uuid "fc55d1bf-5b4e-5900-8dba-dfc04d82fcc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249625} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c87277-896d-50dd-82b5-385c9dad90a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c001b720-cfa8-54e2-8c25-eb8cf8283524" :question/math-academy-id "q-115792" :question/problem "Solve for $w$ where $1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}=\\frac{w+5}{3w}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $w=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $3w,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =\\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w\\cdot (1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =3w\\cdot \\left(\\frac{w+5}{3w}\\right) \\\\ 3w\\cdot 1-3w\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =3w\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w-\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{\\cancel{3w}} & =\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{\\cancel{3w}} \\\\ 3w-(2w-3) & =w+5 \\\\ 3w-2w+3 & =w+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $w,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-2w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w-w & =5-3 \\\\ 0 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} {:answer/id #uuid "284a2d2d-aec2-56b2-a83a-447fe88b345e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247782} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd2d177-bb80-5fbb-b4fb-239d54aa4023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248101} {:answer/id #uuid "9a88c5c8-97bc-5272-bbaa-7a0f74513dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248802} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeb1cf3-0a3b-5e0b-81f5-f88d8973a54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d68dd-7d98-50ea-b0e6-bb084af9ebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a05ffdc-3b52-5cee-9869-369fd44c61d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115794" :question/problem "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $z=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3z$ and $6z.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3z,6z)=6z.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z} \\\\ 6z\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2z-1}{6z}\\right) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ 6z\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2z-1}{6z}\\right) & =6z\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3z-1}{3z}\\right)+6z\\cdot \\left(\\frac{z-1}{6z}\\right) \\\\ \\cancel{6z}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3z}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3z-1}{\\cancel{3z}}\\right)+\\cancel{6z}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}\\right) \\\\ 2z-1 & =2\\cdot (3z-1)+z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-1 & =2(3z-1)+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =6z-2+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =7z-3 \\\\ 2z-7z & =-3+1 \\\\ -5z & =-2 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=\\frac{2}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3092b847-9a1c-5a74-ba95-b4b2f9e20e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247864} {:answer/id #uuid "482af19d-4556-5055-adeb-60a4908cc520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{5}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248070} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7a4e45-a2e3-5501-9782-368cda05897e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248280} {:answer/id #uuid "66c404c0-5d65-5f82-8143-eb62e139cab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248347} {:answer/id #uuid "8788d875-3f8a-5c3c-a4f6-64b613897277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247864} :answer-field/id #uuid "01618e3c-d36d-57e5-80f1-a722d4a7cfe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9fbd632-5a91-5de9-9554-86c175581e67" :question/math-academy-id "q-115810" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3-x}{4x-5}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-x}{4x-5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}),\\,(\\frac{5}{4},3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})$ | $(\\frac{5}{4},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $4x-5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc41766-0986-51e8-b091-2bcef799f405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248121} {:answer/id #uuid "584d6e66-71f7-5a39-a091-953905fa6aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248224} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbe32e0-af0b-5def-a65f-dadf0db7bfbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248269} {:answer/id #uuid "96cb5ecd-e868-52e8-98b5-8888aef2dcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248767} {:answer/id #uuid "9862d875-7d98-5eb0-8fa6-7e40fb6a5e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248783} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d92c6f4-4085-5042-85f7-219bb47ed314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08760f63-a3a6-5971-af6c-dce5f1758637" :question/math-academy-id "q-115836" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)(2-x)}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)(2-x)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptote at $x=2.$  The root and the asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{2},2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{5}{2},2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+3$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive in the interval $(-\\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52745624-72e9-5677-99cd-0e87ea32405f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248164} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8251a7-b60c-5682-bba1-dda5dadef9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248245} {:answer/id #uuid "66fdaa04-dbf3-5d34-b5c1-3f29b2f673a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248348} {:answer/id #uuid "7179694e-44fa-59ac-98b3-2287e31fe0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248416} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6cd28f-9953-5764-8b20-750fe6c69004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248416} :answer-field/id #uuid "d32eb108-3213-5815-96ae-0aa38dae73ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4093859b-39de-5aab-ae6c-e0a99b8d2362" :question/math-academy-id "q-115852" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e065edec23179a946ea28aa6f2b4f9a439ba23c014c38dded0cc7c756867a90.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](images/5b/5b92aa0ecaf7dd906a39c44e31a12e725802d9c7c5cdc046351030ae04f6809e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x+1)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115a7de1-284d-5a58-abff-53bd111a48ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247580} {:answer/id #uuid "3214e581-5c88-55be-a0a0-97f2386bfb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247882} {:answer/id #uuid "759b6264-92ec-50fe-8363-fa2b2ce89d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248459} {:answer/id #uuid "8031624f-fad6-5b1a-a046-4c29579ab011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248552} {:answer/id #uuid "d51c1911-024b-58fb-a554-5ab224923919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x-4}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248459} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9214767-ec8e-5e07-9c88-4008821ace99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9285d6ff-fcc8-5fe1-ab97-90ac8bf0cd24" :question/math-academy-id "q-115854" :question/problem "![](images/75/753554f8129ab0ed83f19e5cadefba27cb3b700818bd3a3f801c64aaf01ded70.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f2/f26e5b61c573664f0397667aff82cfe5d918e0d22f005658862c28c53d5de748.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=2$ and $x=4.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-4)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(x-2)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "306ecb3c-eaf4-5059-99dd-e7da1b4c6d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247862} {:answer/id #uuid "4b93b821-b6c9-5943-b6e9-0ea8381beca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248099} {:answer/id #uuid "9c72a601-e6f8-585a-9af0-5454afc293ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248816} {:answer/id #uuid "ac379da2-dff1-5987-8b99-d8499195d1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248959} {:answer/id #uuid "ac60a934-5190-5c39-b209-c7d455b5c195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{7}{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247862} :answer-field/id #uuid "42fb8525-408c-5909-ad81-e66cce4e1e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13d9ccfb-0e87-5a68-8e7f-a26501e83768" :question/math-academy-id "q-115858" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(2x+7)(3-x)}{{x}^{2}}\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x+7)(3-x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{7}{2},0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{7}{2},0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6406ea0c-d398-53da-8121-bc8b8cbef85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461797} {:answer/id #uuid "38fe89af-041f-5f67-a609-1f185814986e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461798} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb11d9b3-0886-59bc-8089-a6919106a383" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd31cd7-9886-5da3-9db1-a3727c748122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461799} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b0c3dd-f33a-57a6-9ef9-034ee3c77321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461799} :answer-field/id #uuid "33dd7160-e17e-53b2-94f0-97450282ace1" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f60d6aa9-be98-5d12-9cf1-725213adda8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461801} {:answer/id #uuid "008761dc-0095-56cd-a692-4b3ee2a31d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461801} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d1396a6-5b69-56e2-8588-8b83ce3f9319" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "df71ed63-f106-5382-97b4-fb9c0eee25d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116040" :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddb340a99b8ab92d60e17184274854ce3d1b1bfa4972a79e74b8bfe9b0a81ce7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/73ba54766cd08a0b596e43cd8da93209e2548c68821e70a44418a24dfdf27a38.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -3.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to -3.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $y\\ne -3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{y}{y+3}=-\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{3}{y})}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0237b559-7d33-5cd0-9da8-8d8b205f4742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247438} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb99e31-a544-5220-80a8-c1892b899c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247705} {:answer/id #uuid "cac77d12-ae34-5ddd-8afb-806b1d42068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249230} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368912-431d-5c1c-94c8-d1e252061d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} :answer-field/id #uuid "05de44ca-2588-5951-97b7-06f8b0b05597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f403d57-f6d2-5901-9ad6-4def1edb7127" :question/math-academy-id "q-116104" :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and ${x}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,{x}^{2})={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3x-1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\right) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}}\\right)-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ (3x-1) & =x\\cdot 2-(x-3) \\\\ (3x-1) & =2x-(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1) & =2x-(x-3) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x-x+3 \\\\ 3x-1 & =x+3 \\\\ 3x-x & =3+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095b621d-d28c-5438-ac86-bb537204c4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247505} {:answer/id #uuid "19938bf6-8fa9-534a-99f3-a34885b9ec72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247656} {:answer/id #uuid "4194167a-a248-589b-8287-fdb853c46d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248008} {:answer/id #uuid "f3500a3c-8d1a-531b-a225-4a4c4972bd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249554} {:answer/id #uuid "f647ed95-e4d5-5ab0-baa1-dad4fc8d3414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249554} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bf22087-6398-52e3-9c0b-1effe99dbc91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3783baa6-61fe-5e5f-a6e5-bd47f3137af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-116106" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2{m}^{2},$ ${m}^{2}$ and $m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,{m}^{2},2{m}^{2})=2{m}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{m}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}} \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{m}\\right)-2{m}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}\\right) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2m\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}}\\right)-\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3m+4}{\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{{m}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{m}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ 2m\\cdot 3-(3m+4) & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6m-(3m+4) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6m-(3m+4) & =2 \\\\ 6m-3m-4 & =2 \\\\ 3m & =6 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "490ebf16-be65-5961-9d0d-cf1630467e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248074} {:answer/id #uuid "5471ceaa-7aa0-522b-bc44-99a2c33e60c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248184} {:answer/id #uuid "5808fda1-1a90-5e08-94cb-9fa5216dac10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248223} {:answer/id #uuid "98055398-5a21-573b-be94-4273933180c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{(y+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248779} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f3cef1-3427-5b71-8299-4d6a8a8c0796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248074} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2994f11-1583-5b56-b5fd-22b3a9a76894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "930070fc-32d3-575b-8b2d-70f53ae4fe30" :question/math-academy-id "q-116177" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f023d50d11e5b6604e5fcc257e35aa4b8615e8c91f5855d10e73293720c27e4.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b6/b6cf036f732d1cdff6f2e575b2ce082979d986416afd195282789303dcf70131.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}$ become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2894b428-0dac-564c-8fcf-27326493d182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,\\frac{1}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247784} {:answer/id #uuid "42b9859a-fa85-54fe-9d67-659813cef0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248019} {:answer/id #uuid "575beb8a-9b3f-5691-bc70-f6a633ab2e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248216} {:answer/id #uuid "9904edc1-89f8-5037-b7fd-2cd0cbe8018d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248790} {:answer/id #uuid "e4dbb32d-b934-5fb1-830e-eb44bcfbe9de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249439} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6592047-e115-5419-805b-d324f7bbcdfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "70d44166-fe43-573d-87ca-1e5612abc3bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-116194" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x & \\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x-4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+4)(2x-1) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+4)(2x-1).$ The roots are $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+4)(2x-1)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+4)(2x-1)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+4)(2x-1)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "05263274-8a30-5b9e-99c8-bc4632800973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{2},6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250593} {:answer/id #uuid "191782ef-158c-5c61-8b5a-53889b26df66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250772} {:answer/id #uuid "217773cc-9a72-5309-917b-175c453ddfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250837} {:answer/id #uuid "27773dbc-82c6-5d97-8262-5945a24bc201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250891} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6f4f87-1ccd-589c-90e1-8890c55c15eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250932} {:answer/id #uuid "4683b3fe-eb8e-5220-9fa6-0a9afcd7c7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251140} {:answer/id #uuid "4755537d-9558-58e3-b1fb-e15adf27ad35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\sin ⁡(x-\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251146} {:answer/id #uuid "62a869bc-7ad8-5514-a78a-02dff1b40c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251359} {:answer/id #uuid "89286d84-c732-59b4-956d-104585bfdb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251711} {:answer/id #uuid "8a541a16-d054-5f31-9f18-54e78bfdbf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251720} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5a5357-7eb2-5b78-bbad-edc691df5fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251758} {:answer/id #uuid "94083696-43f4-54f2-b3f6-a3866a469f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251825} {:answer/id #uuid "a5579b86-e0b3-519e-954e-6b1008604303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251978} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5d4464-861a-5a52-a1a0-0a5e9ed382db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252027} {:answer/id #uuid "b669eb12-c6ef-5ff2-b6e0-4a8a24191e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252160} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e95c7-d45f-56b8-b0c9-fc8dbcb94077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252319} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b8421d-9d4a-5d21-9ad8-60eec16fae69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252341} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f0698c-7c2c-5b62-addb-c963a8f40249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\sin ⁡(x+\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252436} {:answer/id #uuid "e15499e7-0f10-5e04-9058-9c6db2670a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252536} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fd4633-c96e-5bff-9e85-4b2076e4aa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252710} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9086da-236a-59d4-9d03-b14bbfe51a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c68d3b3-bfa0-555e-9fc6-8b730c50fe32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251443} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf7f06a-9b53-512b-b8c5-0df50416db91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251586} {:answer/id #uuid "a83cbc29-6959-534c-8462-e8290b065b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252006} {:answer/id #uuid "da65b8c8-95da-52e8-bb16-41f74f364106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252471} {:answer/id #uuid "dac0614e-6504-5379-bcfb-b3e716fa8777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251586} :answer-field/id #uuid "211439b1-9068-5be0-80c4-19cfce9bdea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dab1908a-ed96-5f67-a8b2-41493fe7dc98" :question/math-academy-id "q-116296" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,5]=[1,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/8f/8fe84e0056eaa8320b4824740a259226b1a18ff1b64ba648d8740d6cac8703fc.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(1)+f(2)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{1}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{1\\text{st trapezoid}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(2)+f(5)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{2}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+3}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{3+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+15 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "735748ee-a8f4-5955-aec8-129869926ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251503} {:answer/id #uuid "96039b1d-86e0-535a-af86-b71cbf7de115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251841} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aa686a-3313-54ed-b39d-439cefa4f903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252129} {:answer/id #uuid "bbac8a44-c6c3-53c8-99e6-61569aea30f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252204} {:answer/id #uuid "fb596aa9-597f-5870-80b0-3ef5bbe6d687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252758} :answer-field/id #uuid "30e03666-5a92-5627-a4e9-d4f579effe33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6215941f-91c2-5319-8cbc-f79483d60df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-116300" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-3,2]=[-3,-1]\\cup [-1,0]\\cup [0,2],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $6$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/25/25dc419eb5711b0d990909c611a6eacb2754285fb45dc0d969207e63dc09ba12.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(-3)+f(-1)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{1}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{1\\text{st trapezoid}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(-1)+f(0)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{2}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(0)+f(2)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{3}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (-1-(-3))+\\frac{4+8}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+6+14 \\\\  & =26\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0008821a-eb35-5a25-b9b1-0c2a29881e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250552} {:answer/id #uuid "63d6f21d-4bd8-57ca-bde5-2c278fa01d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251367} {:answer/id #uuid "8da18724-5cab-54f6-bc25-b97c4f7e5b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251751} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47b14c-7529-5e46-a36b-b961f8ed005a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252216} {:answer/id #uuid "eaeecfbd-4dcc-5ba0-a2b6-86ce4e22ad7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250552} :answer-field/id #uuid "586037f5-a354-5dfd-a5c2-7a91bdcf07ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19296d4e-02f5-5e1d-85b0-8bf76523ed9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-116504" :question/problem "Determine ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=3x-5$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(3x-5)-1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5145d4f1-d512-59f7-a758-57cea003a8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251218} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ae047b-0d7e-5a5a-910d-778ce18374ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251940} {:answer/id #uuid "a6fe3e11-8f4d-5e2d-b453-7453f1a9af01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251992} {:answer/id #uuid "b60be8c4-0123-5ff1-95cb-f0f4c3a98252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252155} {:answer/id #uuid "fa884147-45f9-5a95-82ca-e41f0f78da77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252155} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcd9004-c1db-5281-8532-d9d0a50cdf40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bd492a5-90b9-5814-b28f-73caeabd307e" :question/math-academy-id "q-116967" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e32b5de-8f31-5a1a-b63e-e365b9fee60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250677} {:answer/id #uuid "73f7a2e5-e2b2-593a-a9b1-35167c3462cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251509} {:answer/id #uuid "882ba387-aff7-59be-b7f4-413e1338a6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251700} {:answer/id #uuid "91e96c13-9e62-5631-818c-7405ac0cfee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251800} {:answer/id #uuid "94440cd6-656d-5a8d-a05b-1a5328c585b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251509} :answer-field/id #uuid "f548b21f-1759-584c-8575-1bcc88892046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "767c1d10-353b-5f6e-af12-3fd3742fad7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-116970" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a679d9e-0792-5d29-a824-a9f73ea6726d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250645} {:answer/id #uuid "34ee0515-ea9b-5ae4-8f82-311045f9ed2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250996} {:answer/id #uuid "63ddf3da-57a0-51c7-91e5-39b41774fda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251369} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab163e4-9c6e-56cb-922f-db4c82d2f150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251726} {:answer/id #uuid "91773a10-a64b-51fd-acda-3336e3078ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250645} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b89ed6-5b84-5dd8-98c3-6b9eca8fc5c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e44482f-7e95-5fde-99c9-d20711dd6ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-116971" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ is in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d227fa2-68c3-597d-89d1-06a7facb732e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250668} {:answer/id #uuid "17151923-edaf-5b23-902d-bf8520272a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250754} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3cc75f-d171-5730-941e-aea038aa422a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251325} {:answer/id #uuid "7540230e-07cc-5888-b7ad-1c4229a06309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251519} {:answer/id #uuid "9afc2ba5-bfe1-5f60-a3a4-94561492ca36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251884} :answer-field/id #uuid "606869d9-c681-53fe-ac88-e5e6a21f3294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86245648-d363-527e-bfea-ec2dc9e748ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-116974" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ is in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0a69c3-de2a-5629-9e9a-01e7bf9e2c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461809} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3ff2c1-b103-5029-a96c-9f3d809db7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461809} :answer-field/id #uuid "909a604a-0da9-52b3-8331-6540b6b46528" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc89b79-e7f9-5da0-89dc-18cb255ffce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461811} {:answer/id #uuid "a12c7112-b5c5-5054-816c-47769741fe0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461812}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461811} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ecaba8-e054-56b0-984d-74d7c129549e" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462659} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb00d1b4-e83c-5829-af51-92502125af82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461813} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3ad07f-1650-57aa-add0-1865e9384e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461813} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff580f36-fed7-5993-a941-ba77d516a454" :answer-field/key "$(1,\\pi )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e28d53-5d38-56c2-9699-b35e1120ccc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667878} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6c193b-3618-5ca6-a17e-89e0c99b4c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186667879} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d3b5fb4-bd3e-5d13-85f5-de75e70b45e0" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a9ed09ea-d30a-5142-9dec-aca74736564e" :question/math-academy-id "q-117025" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d30c4f2-1c9b-56aa-b62b-7e9a63a5c394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250936} {:answer/id #uuid "51ebda4a-c6ab-56ec-a85d-515c28547d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251224} {:answer/id #uuid "9cde4a0d-d36b-53dd-9c07-256ac68ea24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251906} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b65520-eb87-5289-bc62-41aa3b67fb30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252285} {:answer/id #uuid "df53c346-071e-5d55-b02f-a149afdcd833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252522} :answer-field/id #uuid "311dd4be-59af-5907-99cb-162e30c5bc61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93ab0f69-1e36-5855-80b5-7cee6cddd602" :question/math-academy-id "q-117102" :question/problem "![](images/af/afd2f435a60fbb5a0f0f83c5f8965a74ca107d1ed4b14d03401f7966a323d2f1.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(2,4)$ and $Y(2,0),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, we can find the center using the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{2+2}{2},\\frac{4+0}{2})=(2,2)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(2,2),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dbf520-5c3b-5b0b-ba2f-9f13812d437b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250862} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2c3273-1427-5200-b8fa-ae62dd0254b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252224} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d74c25-4afb-5a05-86ec-741a51b80fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252690} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f229e0-e36c-5d89-bfa3-eb4e80db5b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252700} {:answer/id #uuid "fb945b04-2597-5034-872d-2f63ecdf08d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250862} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee3ff0f-0ad3-57b0-80be-ce3303f10b7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7556293-0fd9-5aac-b946-74962289d2c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117103" :question/problem "![](images/08/08653e0cdc9bb404bd7ef193dfc9f1b5c0884002e83dd72cbcbda5779a2363a1.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(2,-1)$ and $Y(2,1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(-1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, we can find the center using the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{2+2}{2},\\frac{-1+1}{2})=(2,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=1$ and center $(a,b)=(2,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={1}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1449a778-d0cd-54af-b254-ed3e6cf1e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250729} {:answer/id #uuid "210a0d60-0ad2-5c82-bbdf-57fa4f773dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250833} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbdbf00-5f32-5f31-b62b-8082aac14eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251080} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa1cebc-2118-57ee-afa4-b243d9a8d486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251881} {:answer/id #uuid "c79d3915-b972-5b9e-9dbd-ac4105903344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252320} :answer-field/id #uuid "8058d059-e858-5260-9048-73a64f45f512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7713e957-0cbe-5d3f-93d1-81e2b678b8a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117105" :question/problem "![](images/05/0598fca48db1dbfb0cacadcf2ffb5cd987fc4e922fe85585849763fedac1f28e.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-3,0)$ and $(1,-2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-3,0)$ and $Y(1,-2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-3-1)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-3+1}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-1,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and center $(a,b)=(-1,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27146995-40b8-5fb9-a436-de880c16f96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250890} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3b83a1-785f-569a-9e04-1f4d911a200b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251061} {:answer/id #uuid "6e015f74-e5e1-5e7d-8695-c6dc5c93e9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251454} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ee6e0-b5ff-59bc-82f8-2de8c7c6342b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251762} {:answer/id #uuid "b43bb4a8-cb77-5ae2-870d-f55b9e5f7c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252136} :answer-field/id #uuid "94017d7f-a209-5ab8-99c1-91d78574ffbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6610e1ab-73a7-5f4a-8c57-8d6e77beff9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-117106" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a0820b21672bbf16d2fdb35d708aefc4129fdc81c7594b55af3bd2ef5b8fc0f.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-2,0)$ and $Y(0,2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(-1,1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and center $(a,b)=(-1,1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b1554c-96e1-5f34-bd7d-39439e7d2d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250994} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0c96c8-b6f3-508c-bbce-383fbffc5d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251069} {:answer/id #uuid "71463607-983f-5491-bdcc-71080d1290aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251480} {:answer/id #uuid "78ba35b2-ca4d-559a-9e58-c33761c27a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251553} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfbf876-ab7e-5bb9-b30b-9654f0019146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250994} :answer-field/id #uuid "233384c8-214c-54f0-a58a-ee2651d7fa9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b6f2966b-20a6-5c34-af5a-1e6c31f128aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-117107" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f1573d17cb9a603727b644da6598355875b0be4b8c43a382e562155cb7a095c.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-1,-3)$ and $(3,1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-1,-3)$ and $Y(3,1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-1+3}{2},\\frac{-3+1}{2})=(1,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ and center $(a,b)=(1,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4364b684-2b7d-5e18-879b-f994b51d9bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461821} {:answer/id #uuid "726b0f10-b494-52ff-ab4f-cbd77583f245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461822} :answer-field/id #uuid "514ec5e1-0581-5679-a933-05b4b2c5e1b1" :answer-field/key "$(1,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd5716d-9dbf-5bc7-8cf8-538f510e0db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461823} {:answer/id #uuid "99ff6521-19de-50df-ba5e-84a3b8910b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461823} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e716a7b-bdc5-5700-b68e-816b38149906" :answer-field/key "$(0,\\pi )$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e75a3248-39d5-58b4-9fe8-de3e292ec18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461825} {:answer/id #uuid "1959566f-a03f-5fb8-9912-7cc79151a8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461826} :answer-field/id #uuid "190c877c-8abf-5abc-b367-a2eaa1bf59ed" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "90a74c1f-7bff-59cd-a30f-99c5f38ea504" :question/math-academy-id "q-117250" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178238d9-e0d1-5777-b3cf-766b12d670d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250761} {:answer/id #uuid "229202ef-99c0-5de5-8945-e3c5a7eb006f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250847} {:answer/id #uuid "911d51bd-b9f6-5cb4-82f6-2441b4de6b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251790} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e556a6-bb11-5ba5-a261-04fe9a60f028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252573} {:answer/id #uuid "e963bf4d-65b7-5ce4-8bd3-da7c8dac957c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250847} :answer-field/id #uuid "194878cb-3d94-5092-8cec-b3676b597b31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "59cdd50b-76d3-5bd3-b113-bd6f2cfed6e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-117289" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}((\\sqrt{x})^{4}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}({1}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2217da09-6925-529f-a47e-fd42bf90ef04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250844} {:answer/id #uuid "45ac8701-cd2f-526f-9ebd-a915c88cd734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251130} {:answer/id #uuid "8d540679-4d39-51d3-a6dc-4648abcea14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251748} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b80351-56c5-523b-aca4-c1c6f8888cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252116} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6871dc-a56c-5f61-876e-28ff32cdd63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252116} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e9f462-0c94-5801-b600-a9497a75e105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8386e6c5-c0be-5d00-a9a0-00a17c58167d" :question/math-academy-id "q-117334" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584fac91-8bae-5744-a7dd-3edc6e568663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251277} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3b4455-892e-5cb0-b55f-c02362f7dd51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251451} {:answer/id #uuid "9b630d65-3745-5709-a4d8-99a051f2f992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251891} {:answer/id #uuid "b0639662-48e7-5792-8c11-4193bcb89415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252094} {:answer/id #uuid "ffba56af-778c-5b31-948d-2fac50ec6dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251277} :answer-field/id #uuid "0253ed24-eb48-5557-8ad6-d36fc78547f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ac39f28-cd79-5e1b-a032-0394adfaac51" :question/math-academy-id "q-117336" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047b4668-4730-5221-97ec-c1622216bf25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250587} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0b3750-daa8-506d-a95d-b239cc2bebff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251323} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2a0acf-96c1-523d-bc9c-91d85cf265f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251460} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f3581d-17bc-54c3-a8fb-ab993d012ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252459} {:answer/id #uuid "e46d5a72-59c4-5cb2-9a6d-4188dae7bc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252556}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251460} :answer-field/id #uuid "049029ed-ad58-5a9b-99d6-3aabc7cfcd1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8315c50b-3e6d-51ba-b8bf-054ce4b2ac93" :question/math-academy-id "q-117338" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is not in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6e6535f-fc18-5a57-b91a-57b92fa14ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461845} {:answer/id #uuid "f25b54e1-966f-571e-842a-af41665f8071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461846} :answer-field/id #uuid "29680fbe-167d-53aa-b0de-3cc2028c3df9" :answer-field/key "$(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9e011aa-8908-5d07-8aac-da3595692e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461847} {:answer/id #uuid "eef90e09-14d8-5319-9747-8d4ee5b49e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461848}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461848} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab08439f-3b3d-5c5b-a724-9dea82728cfb" :answer-field/key "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462677} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257f0459-756d-5bef-93c8-2315439df9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461849} {:answer/id #uuid "2a309c64-ac52-5561-ac65-e3710790558e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461850} :answer-field/id #uuid "df96a148-3ab6-53a8-bfb3-0111bf03f0d5" :answer-field/key "$(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bfbb710f-9bcf-5482-b898-5f45cf7ceff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117366" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c78a2d78-de5b-55db-9598-b2738d47cf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461851} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2cfab7-7117-5fc2-99af-f66e7b541971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461852}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461852} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58d20dc-9c7d-500c-a86a-f3f1271fa49f" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94823d85-e3b3-52c9-9f56-e5542e922a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461853} {:answer/id #uuid "24ead7ad-8485-5ded-ae26-9d6c7d203b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461853} :answer-field/id #uuid "809ec92f-116c-5ca0-acee-614fc750aad8" :answer-field/key "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8e8f20-54be-52d5-9909-13466cd4de2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461855} {:answer/id #uuid "f79a093b-71bd-5e31-bb2f-d45dcebd2e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461856} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fce65dd-a328-5462-9d11-7b16710dbaba" :answer-field/key "$(0,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d01f8dbc-d72d-57bf-bd1d-25c6d02854e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117371" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "629f362a-07cf-5131-b149-b6fc1e0c58ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251357} {:answer/id #uuid "700c2728-d3d3-5572-94d5-b40f2ae63720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251468} {:answer/id #uuid "d2032432-4b16-5cf7-abdd-e24558719f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252408} {:answer/id #uuid "de546f21-a506-5006-8322-3cbc1cd3fca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252510} {:answer/id #uuid "df83d57c-1c50-571c-b518-d9250cbcdcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252525} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7bf2c88-b79f-50e2-9458-7e7d4dea41cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "09dc5170-6e81-5e4c-903a-ed41666f4efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-117441" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{3}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-1,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](images/0d/0d7fcf48509464cdd6c22962b11c3db4fe24a08fa285adeeb9e30114c9667f49.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-1,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-([\\frac{1}{4}-1]-[\\frac{1}{4}+1]) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,3],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{81}{4}-3]-[\\frac{1}{4}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{69}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=2+18=20.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e104022-bb4e-565d-a6bf-bd962d901ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251070} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7c7f6e-d175-5ced-b466-3c36c4f17838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251317} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ea3bf9-1407-5df8-8f30-0caede0decbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252322} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e6f228-8f8a-57cb-9af2-282ce558e2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252406} {:answer/id #uuid "eef4021b-6b37-5e54-b681-f27585eee147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251317} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48b917e-69fa-56ff-b770-34926780baa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3b40d2d1-a796-5057-96c3-9515dd6af44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117442" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3{x}^{3}+24$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+24 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{3} & =-24 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-2\\in [-3,-1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n![](images/80/80315b4862adf8ebb1f91f5c4c7606a59dfa5738c40e34179a745e03a0a25693.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-3,-2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot ([4-16]-[\\frac{81}{4}-24]) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-12+\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{33}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{99}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,-1],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot ([\\frac{1}{4}-8]-[4-16]) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{4}+12) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{99}{4}+\\frac{51}{4}=\\frac{75}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f30d4f-ecb8-52ae-8866-b6d2b7a98d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250600} {:answer/id #uuid "253502e5-6b38-5479-8901-deb361427879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250871} {:answer/id #uuid "64cd272b-9535-5134-a77d-f5ca15ed964f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251379} {:answer/id #uuid "b0dbd45d-8af9-5745-946c-4090c6b37a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252100} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bb503b-b594-53e7-a3c7-deb02b9ead9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252404}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252404} :answer-field/id #uuid "659756fd-4676-5210-826a-b2127de62911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e2fabcc-cfa7-5343-adee-3a4f628a6015" :question/math-academy-id "q-117444" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=9-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $3\\in [-3,6]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=6.$\n\n![](images/70/70df9534ea4f69993871888a5f751ecd6aea9d1e0aaaf128d55442d9913b3dd5.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-3,3]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9x{|}_{-3}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =[9(3)-9(-3)]-[\\frac{(3)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =54-18 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [3,6]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9x{|}_{3}^{6}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-9[6-3]+[\\frac{(6)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =-27+63 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=36+36=72.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221d3b5f-cc70-5be5-bbbb-88b48563c84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250845} {:answer/id #uuid "39f102aa-8409-5e6b-bfe5-29a4d81581ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251032} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7e15df-b52c-53d7-9f92-3d20507b6493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251771} {:answer/id #uuid "c24bd90d-8ca1-5648-896b-d39aa1d0b02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{50}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252268} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d9ade2-7e98-5e81-9c7f-87218a08b141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250845} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f6089d-8cdf-5bcc-832d-cf7b9d5476a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1dd372c9-9e4e-560b-b302-4bd8a1bb83b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117454" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{5}+{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{3}+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=-1$ are our real solutions. Since $-1\\in [-2,0]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc57ac72bdc0d496dc9e106c87d9c5f60cd5d8ca11d150f061edda690082cc71.png)\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{6}]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-1}^{0}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}]+[0-\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{49}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{25}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05263274-8a30-5b9e-99c8-bc4632800973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{2},6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250593} {:answer/id #uuid "26c93fc6-e7c9-527e-b808-8e8ae2deae84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250886} {:answer/id #uuid "622a263a-6ba2-5b98-88d5-099953d3f597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251350} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5a5357-7eb2-5b78-bbad-edc691df5fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251758} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9086da-236a-59d4-9d03-b14bbfe51a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252777} :answer-field/id #uuid "49dfe913-70a3-528c-b8ca-29e7f976f6c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8647f1c-5367-5a91-bc69-e78441ce9412" :question/math-academy-id "q-117463" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(6,7)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}-12x-14\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32673930-9f4a-5254-90a4-683608db94ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250980} {:answer/id #uuid "37c42776-909b-5ef6-815f-da6db5453064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251013} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7d65b5-ff3c-5cc0-b550-7efd3f240b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251445} {:answer/id #uuid "73bee433-2d27-51f0-8ea6-e77da9e58d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251506} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a4e96-192d-564f-90fd-dca29f7f969c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250980} :answer-field/id #uuid "be90ed80-3fb6-5d21-8795-61bcc98ca73e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ff20307-ce27-5015-aa4b-6179740ecdbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117482" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{4} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/48/488788313bd32999611a520ec26e4ba6fd68a56e0333a7252a0faaf7107cad90.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16\\cdot y{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot (2-(-2))-(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =64-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13908070-27e4-55b6-b9d8-747ae31aa53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250723} {:answer/id #uuid "6092f928-5d89-5252-a2d6-19742c141c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251343} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ef3c84-c861-5040-a544-3c158c2fb43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252325} {:answer/id #uuid "c9504c0c-6404-571e-91b7-9add25bffd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252334} {:answer/id #uuid "e081921a-0bfa-5de5-8331-106f7bdc3b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252334} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d15094c-346d-5dd8-beeb-65fe9070d24a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad970949-6b8c-5c6c-9a59-687628bd497c" :question/math-academy-id "q-117485" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(9-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/bb/bbcf218e7b6181c861ba4c804357cc53ae6004453914259a9fe98ad5f09ac4d4.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0)-(\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}-\\frac{81}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240d2ccc-ecc1-5478-b890-31522a2717e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250866} {:answer/id #uuid "79aaaef9-a2f7-5d5c-abb8-5b85bb1fdea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251564} {:answer/id #uuid "b44a023e-15d1-5325-81a6-5fd4a82ac424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252137} {:answer/id #uuid "fc99263c-bf3e-5203-9dfc-14d886d67e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252767} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd85827-d00a-536b-8339-10a15d307259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250866} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5f85f7-6885-56a5-876c-dfe7f6fa5dd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4c72108d-e895-553c-9eb8-17579eed6bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-117487" :question/problem "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x={y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-6y+9) & =0 \\\\ y(y-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/75/7509bf6a10838458b110d982740c1399aa36279b9ce4a231f99b76d7c7cee0b3.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x={y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}({y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}-6\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}+9\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4}-0)-6(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-0)+9(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}-54+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e407b00-43bc-5c4b-a5e5-3d9577735776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250950} {:answer/id #uuid "63f0aa4a-c55d-5a18-ae5c-ee4005c1d0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251372} {:answer/id #uuid "8b04dd44-f950-53dd-94a1-6d2dbab90f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251727} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bbe0dc-3bc8-5d52-a74d-a21a13f5efad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251966} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb36857-b59b-51ca-ac72-b2c0438fb869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251727} :answer-field/id #uuid "2015087a-90cf-5c25-aabe-d7944268845d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "277bb7fd-8284-514b-90e8-ffc120633d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-117546" :question/problem "![](images/91/91ead412d3e333fd4bfbcce785813a530e66ca39858ca108e9920d349443bb53.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point where the line $-2x+3y=12$ intersects the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $-2x+3y=12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x}{3}+4\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{2x}{3}+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+4+1)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+5)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{3}+25 & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{26x}{3}+13 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43205661-f460-56f1-adb1-c7d5bd58eadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251116} {:answer/id #uuid "72935ca7-a8c2-5ce6-aa26-184f0bd6b12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251495} {:answer/id #uuid "ac96fb00-96df-5ad0-90c8-7963646933e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252057} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1ac960-b896-5229-8bcd-ecd0c03b1345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252489} {:answer/id #uuid "efa437e0-6950-5962-9ef4-226f957d0c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252489} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b31603-abee-57c3-8b05-f7502520d3ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e84da28-f1c2-5f42-8b28-8c5abbfd888a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117569" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{4})?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$ \n\nSince $A=1,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|1|=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4161544d-5503-509c-a919-47b75546d693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251094} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0b8f2d-8bf9-5548-96dd-fe899f5d298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252036} {:answer/id #uuid "ba23df8f-a679-5d5c-bde8-2b7238d24224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252191} {:answer/id #uuid "c9834705-9dcd-5c24-afb6-f095959807d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} {:answer/id #uuid "f25fe0b5-3a2d-5c1e-b5a3-425fbf7a30c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} :answer-field/id #uuid "918ffa31-2155-56a2-9928-59d9d880148e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53685d44-d788-5019-bc63-97c441bd2453" :question/math-academy-id "q-117578" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71fbeca3-4760-5d6a-856c-a573ea317eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{300}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251486} {:answer/id #uuid "9229fa93-9ffb-5473-9b2e-ff4e454a6679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251803} {:answer/id #uuid "ca888844-ac33-5b63-a641-8bce7947f0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{480}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252347} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fdabb1-db44-594d-8452-62e77cc9289f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252685} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9b6765-ab75-54b3-a097-f8617bfd508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251803} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf475618-9da6-5ade-89c7-309940393d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e0d5cec2-04d5-542c-903e-28cdf3f5b313" :question/math-academy-id "q-117579" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3}-{45}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{360}^{∘}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}={270}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d740ebb-367e-5b13-abb3-4aaa7aaffcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251327} {:answer/id #uuid "76a2175d-f7a4-5539-8d5c-3821bd16c125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251531} {:answer/id #uuid "a02621c3-5f65-5847-b218-aabe23216fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251935} {:answer/id #uuid "cce190ce-0319-5382-a9e1-b39ca5b6c857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252359} {:answer/id #uuid "e26fda5d-8dbd-578f-ba57-15366c54f032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252359} :answer-field/id #uuid "b25237fb-1e27-5e8c-8801-00721dc99e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6c40bb9-f96b-5776-9878-956cda7f4ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117602" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144e061b-f73d-5be3-bda1-391058d55a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250730} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a460e1-4643-5dfa-b7a9-75bf3c8c4e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251938} {:answer/id #uuid "c003d3cd-0f41-5a46-b2c9-cbd69f66b165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252250} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcd41a4-a8eb-56e3-8276-f008b29e658d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252354} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4e08b0-56cb-5b5d-8d7e-95cd841af3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251938} :answer-field/id #uuid "19bba1e2-852b-5206-914d-d4b8e50e01c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07bc26ba-e0fc-5df0-aee6-46276118de95" :question/math-academy-id "q-117604" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $$\n\\displaystyle y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is any integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6315d9-4c5b-52e3-98df-900d3c444852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250644} {:answer/id #uuid "50caab44-146e-5114-bb63-a98abf44bbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251216} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8a89f1-cd1e-5d53-97df-4d4822adf932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251602} {:answer/id #uuid "8cef8dea-f51e-5b0b-821f-4305afcb5ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251744} {:answer/id #uuid "a7938484-56c7-5ea3-8eac-d9b734d000ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251602} :answer-field/id #uuid "f788a829-1171-5925-a3c0-50bd4a554edb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21457735-cc06-5dc4-85da-719f77cfb1e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-117609" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x & =-\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5},$ where $n$ is any integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09230c2b-35c0-5a36-9160-9f0daa3b36b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250637} {:answer/id #uuid "1e714897-72f2-5c1f-8935-3f00964f5dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250811} {:answer/id #uuid "726065b5-fd75-59b0-b83d-b2afdee0cd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\pi +6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251491} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e844aa-154c-5e78-a224-059ea53b1484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\pi +n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252668} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f5f78a-5bb4-5c83-9ffa-7432ae54ee4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\pi +2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251491} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6c90ae-042e-54a3-bcce-6da95623af0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad397087-e235-5fd2-b060-371e8c5fcb6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117612" :question/problem "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =3\\pi +6n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=3\\pi +6n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df5b9cb-741a-518e-a027-a2c605a7d59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250808} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb08d0d-127f-560e-b6e3-b50f574fc496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251074} {:answer/id #uuid "5f04c1ae-abb0-5a1d-8c5b-6b487347ddc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251336} {:answer/id #uuid "c37bf204-a802-52c4-b1b5-4bf50fca941c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252281} {:answer/id #uuid "db97fe7c-3e00-5d74-9299-16db02da5823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250808} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b74a002-38a9-5485-81eb-2284af18b5b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f82af0ab-61c0-554b-921b-23d086807960" :question/math-academy-id "q-117613" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =2n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c14508-539b-565f-b8a5-0d892cf7edce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250706} {:answer/id #uuid "44f26219-d007-5fb7-a545-3cf6e9219cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251124} {:answer/id #uuid "58054fea-79b4-5f14-be75-7bee99b5c4e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251272} {:answer/id #uuid "b35ee62d-d0c6-5efe-8255-ee1cebed043e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252124} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b7768a-8be4-5c87-9159-6bcf78f5b5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250706} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f3285ca-ab7c-5ddb-932e-ae17f2b7273c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b5bbefb-2661-5ebb-a901-2b55b10c8d0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-117618" :question/problem "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =2n\\pi +\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b5689b-f400-5c59-91b9-4b18a37afbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250830} {:answer/id #uuid "3573e7ec-2c34-5776-b7df-4005b8952d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250998} {:answer/id #uuid "359a89d0-195d-5e1a-a6e8-c79e0baed932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251000} {:answer/id #uuid "478e699b-9423-592e-aa72-2a69e585de19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251147} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4c9f46-f85a-5df2-b676-fcaf2556f358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252746} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e730e0-2e35-5fd5-8112-32b3bf40e6d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "456d74c0-0d84-59eb-8b19-aaac9fb01d5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-117633" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}-5w}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{w(w-5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w-5,\\,3w,\\,w(w-5))=3w(w-5).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=0$ and $w=5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{w(w-5)} \\\\ 3w(w-5)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}) & =3w(w-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{w(w-5)} \\\\ \\frac{6w(w-5)}{w-5}-\\frac{3w(w-5)}{3w}=\\frac{3w(w-5)}{w(w-5)} \\\\ \\frac{6w\\cancel{(w-5)}}{\\cancel{w-5}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3w}(w-5)}{\\cancel{3w}}=\\frac{3\\cancel{w(w-5)}}{\\cancel{w(w-5)}} \\\\ 6w-(w-5) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w-(w-5) & =3 \\\\ 5w+5 & =3 \\\\ 5w & =-2 \\\\ w & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0$ and $-\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 5,$ we conclude that $w=-\\frac{2}{5}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $w=-\\frac{2}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d54320d-83e7-5c15-976a-d302ef8818b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250798} {:answer/id #uuid "481f4e95-85dd-5908-8e90-b7dbff85b0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251152} {:answer/id #uuid "79cd141d-0314-51df-a58d-bd7bfd855dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251566} {:answer/id #uuid "87aa364b-04b2-5b16-8ca0-fc7b276c6b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251697} {:answer/id #uuid "d5719011-952c-5735-a928-42da7fb33319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252443} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f922f9f-3ff9-52c6-a427-f79472b284d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b13a932-fbde-51f3-817a-80b8a854ae44" :question/math-academy-id "q-117634" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}=\\frac{2}{2{V}^{2}+V}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}=\\frac{2}{V(2V+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2V+1,\\,V,\\,V(2V+1))=V(2V+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=0$ and $V=-\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V} & =\\frac{2}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ V(2V+1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}) & =V(2V+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ \\frac{2V(2V+1)}{2V+1}-\\frac{V(2V+1)}{V} & =\\frac{2V(2V+1)}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ \\frac{2V\\cancel{(2V+1)}}{\\cancel{2V+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{V}(2V+1)}{\\cancel{V}} & =\\frac{2\\cancel{V(2V+1)}}{\\cancel{V(2V+1)}} \\\\ 2V-(2V+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2V-(2V+1) & =2 \\\\ 2V-2V-1 & =2 \\\\ -1 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b11be4-3658-52c8-8bf0-25fdceb40fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3ea915-9740-5a98-a33d-698d64c00a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251179} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4e7811-a45b-5e5c-98c0-b14ff8d1be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251199} {:answer/id #uuid "9d77d381-d721-506e-9675-3e44eb48d740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251912} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb9a8c-18f6-586a-9cc9-426dc5df378c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ed92ee-7925-517a-a895-7f73c4059a69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50009c8b-7a58-5669-927f-169a6eff91b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117658" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3}\\right)+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3}\\right)+4=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=3,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2430686f-6354-5ae3-a56a-86797016a196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250867} {:answer/id #uuid "84c30401-b464-5d98-8a9b-267f533cca26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251667} {:answer/id #uuid "97869399-0a57-5928-b446-b55279c01911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} {:answer/id #uuid "a5606c09-671a-5dc4-abd2-15172f36984e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251979} {:answer/id #uuid "ab67f75e-df9d-5d97-89f6-287ab3d2483f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5b3662-7c4a-554a-a3fa-0abb3a6d9754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc8f3fd2-a7ad-59ae-81ee-947a33e17f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-117661" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4}\\right)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=2,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bba8857-f5e6-56ca-a45f-0812c7b5fb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250655} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd82424-b1e1-5a86-85e5-f1003eea35bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250691} {:answer/id #uuid "217c8a93-2ee3-52a5-97c4-77883744d167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250838} {:answer/id #uuid "651cc068-f529-591e-8f88-8198c782bc73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251381} {:answer/id #uuid "e21a8e7e-c773-5560-8961-2b86bea36b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252542} :answer-field/id #uuid "a44e7945-8592-5206-8097-a756b928714c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "edabc5ea-7962-5769-b1ba-f1b3b2333238" :question/math-academy-id "q-117662" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3x+5, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+13, & x\\ge 3\\end{cases}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}(\\frac{1}{3}x+13)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+5x){|}_{-1}^{3}+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+13x){|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =[3\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+5(3)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-5(-1)]+[\\frac{{9}^{2}}{6}+13(9)-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{6}-13(3)] \\\\  & =[12+20]+[12+78] \\\\  & =32+90 \\\\  & =122.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d4a48c7-18c0-5b27-a8e0-623931f72950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250797} {:answer/id #uuid "2a26043f-1a74-5d46-ab72-245ac72a1903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250913} {:answer/id #uuid "713355a4-0b80-5592-878a-5c71e15597d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251479} {:answer/id #uuid "776895cf-ed45-5d0b-b027-d5f1e4d9bb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251539} {:answer/id #uuid "a41c5694-141e-57eb-ad49-4156e0170d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251539} :answer-field/id #uuid "411941eb-2465-5aee-b349-c0c0a6d7e2b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "444a864a-4329-515b-a5d7-ab51253bb56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-117666" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}7-3x, & x<-1 \\\\ 10, & x\\ge -1\\end{cases}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}(7-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(7x-3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{-5}^{-1}+10x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[7(-1)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-7(-5)+3\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}]+10[(1)-(-1)] \\\\  & =[28+36]+10[2] \\\\  & =64+20 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2c7cae-ea40-5d25-94bd-81b21569348a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250949} {:answer/id #uuid "45b06b9e-596b-545e-808c-311375d3aeb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251131} {:answer/id #uuid "8d270e44-4952-547d-985c-e3dc716418ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251745} {:answer/id #uuid "d2523e38-ec7f-5425-9a15-62569fa30a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252413} {:answer/id #uuid "d925a376-d936-563c-891f-0b73af461d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251131} :answer-field/id #uuid "e59725fe-d4eb-538b-a170-f10c28531eaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "54276c2e-46f0-5bfc-a206-23daf7d5a1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-117669" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}1-{x}^{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{cases}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[(0)-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}-(-2)+\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}]+[(2)^{2}+2-(0)^{2}-(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0a04df-4c23-5342-af88-8c60d0bedbd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250818} {:answer/id #uuid "5beada99-8c4e-5421-8d8b-2b770ffd5c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251310} {:answer/id #uuid "706b0f47-8616-5f8b-b1fd-f5bd1edc36ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251471} {:answer/id #uuid "83939783-5919-5fa8-98bd-38a9ade6ee12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251660} {:answer/id #uuid "b58e4e6e-6bac-512f-9635-2b9225fedb75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251660} :answer-field/id #uuid "70dd09d3-4ace-5f60-9d14-433a0944fc8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09fd422a-244d-51a8-a7b3-4942257b716a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117670" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =4n\\pi +\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5bbcb8-b1a1-59af-9e90-55b140550dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250915} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3f502a-cf62-5072-980b-6f9a8c62916f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250938} {:answer/id #uuid "5b69b062-605c-5678-ac45-3d2ae98954bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251307} {:answer/id #uuid "629b53e8-b2aa-55aa-8715-72fe5668cd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251356} {:answer/id #uuid "72594269-cdab-5707-9f35-266beb1ffbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "V=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250915} :answer-field/id #uuid "089cfa26-9dbe-5991-9100-c6c7a6c1ed45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "568a0ae1-5a0c-5cc0-9462-af7e9086fc72" :question/math-academy-id "q-117682" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(V+4),\\,V-4,\\,(V+4)(V-4))=2(V+4)(V-4).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=-4$ and $V=4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4} & =\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ 2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}) & =2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot \\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{V-4} & =\\frac{16(V+4)(V-4)}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(V+4)}(V-4)}{\\cancel{2(V+4)}}+\\frac{2(V+4)\\cancel{(V-4)}}{\\cancel{V-4}} & =\\frac{16\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}}{\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}} \\\\ (V-4)+2(V+4) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(V-4)+2(V+4) & =16 \\\\ V-4+2V+8 & =16 \\\\ 3V & =12 \\\\ V & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $V=4$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f621599-1486-51cc-8798-5435cea2df6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251461} {:answer/id #uuid "809d97c8-9929-5f4b-867e-e1e1c94fbe97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "m=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251635} {:answer/id #uuid "885d0314-5aa8-5885-b994-cc12bef28f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "m=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251702} {:answer/id #uuid "e6833253-d35d-5d86-b5bb-9455ea6698cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "m=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252570} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e43e56-3d56-5413-b69c-00d7701f6053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "m=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252570} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef01d8ee-239f-5582-826d-a723a4095517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "237cb996-b5fc-51c0-b848-df3a338c9048" :question/math-academy-id "q-117687" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+4,\\,m-3,\\,(m+4)(m-3))=(m+4)(m-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-4$ and $m=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3} & =\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ (m+4)(m-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}) & =(m+4)(m-3)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{m+4}+\\frac{(m+4)(m-3)}{m-3} & =\\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)}(m-3)}{\\cancel{m+4}}+\\frac{(m+4)\\cancel{(m-3)}}{\\cancel{m-3}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}}{\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}} \\\\ 3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3 \\\\ 4m-5 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =8 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne -4$ and $2\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $m=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e98401-d6c8-5302-b7e4-7efd03f52f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250851} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad6ea2a-2d99-5a84-95f5-4de824452386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250917} {:answer/id #uuid "b1114f1b-996b-58ba-a377-3714e61345bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252101} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d88197-e2e2-54c0-a94d-703a0e0979b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252705} {:answer/id #uuid "f6850df2-195b-57fd-b2c4-95bd1c631a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250917} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f817ea-b460-5ba0-98d7-64d951dfcab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bbbd0b33-6531-5ae7-87a7-0cd0379d3a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117688" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+3s-4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(s+4),\\,s-1,\\,(s+4)(s-1)))=2(s+4)(s-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $s=-4$ and $s=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(s+4)} & =\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)} \\\\ 2(s+4)(s-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2(s+4)} & =2(s+4)(s-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)}) \\\\ \\frac{2(s+4)(s-1)}{2(s+4)} & =\\frac{2(s+4)(s-1)}{s-1}-\\frac{6(s+4)(s-1)}{(s+4)(s-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(s+4)}(s-1)}{\\cancel{2(s+4)}} & =\\frac{2(s+4)\\cancel{(s-1)}}{\\cancel{s-1}}-\\frac{6\\cancel{(s+4)(s-1)}}{\\cancel{(s+4)(s-1)}} \\\\ (s-1) & =2(s+4)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-1) & =2(s+4)-6 \\\\ s-1 & =2s+2 \\\\ s & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-3\\ne -4$ and $-3\\ne 1,$ we conclude that $s=-3$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $s=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192cc94d-2bdc-5aa5-8395-3038aa3ee599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250773} {:answer/id #uuid "4831ea5f-e09b-527b-af85-515fffa0b3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251154} {:answer/id #uuid "5214d5d3-d621-543f-8d56-e6f7af5a61c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251225} {:answer/id #uuid "65c18de8-50f1-510b-be3e-67e3e14593c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251389} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee22af7-fed1-5570-8b02-e7f32f959eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251389} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c602f0c-b42d-53d5-bc8e-aa3b17f2cc1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a30a0cb-a192-58f4-b341-cb115dd8b12f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117698" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c88fe20-6412-59e2-b1b7-e7cf56cd4232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}+3n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251057} {:answer/id #uuid "482daf94-6ec7-5327-90e5-eb6022e82fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}+n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251153} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf38a51-fa8b-5e31-801a-13334d97e7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}+3n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252033} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d48266-80e6-5e25-96d5-7105d24226f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}+2n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252287} {:answer/id #uuid "c47a29e7-fe5b-5f79-aced-74872a799e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}+n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252033} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c8cef2c-5911-588a-a25f-4c223e0f4d2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5047cf25-b739-562d-83c1-100604cd7d8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-117700" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi x}{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}+3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}+3n,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01818715-3473-5b13-a95c-273b0fd7cf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250567} {:answer/id #uuid "208d16ee-0f1a-5c0c-b441-ec9bbd49065b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250829} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6cc823-f22d-51fb-92a6-c15226e8152e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251769} {:answer/id #uuid "afb957d2-8102-50a3-b3b3-a5be5eab3db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252085} {:answer/id #uuid "f92f2ef3-2d39-5056-9ee8-31e67e5519fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252734}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252085} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea16ba1-248d-58bf-a5ed-f277435f4ea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9df0ed13-fe26-5647-9ee9-26d80317a291" :question/math-academy-id "q-117706" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 4x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a45c300-259c-5043-904a-258c04ed9291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250642} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc96f1b-0e16-545a-bf0e-e9cf025ef5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250672} {:answer/id #uuid "1cec7b71-cab2-56f2-b08a-62812bc2f74c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne 2\\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250796} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6a5af2-17f1-5f58-8f5c-87606713c95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne 2\\pi +2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250810} {:answer/id #uuid "23b1fa8f-07a8-5c82-965c-2ddbe118ac2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250796} :answer-field/id #uuid "13778e5b-4571-5e67-a580-461c4c3ba9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9781ee8c-a2f6-5b72-adf5-6e3e834a8a1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-117707" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function  $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2\\pi +4n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne 2\\pi +4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3940f3a5-0489-51b9-b2bd-b2c9156756a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251026} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6d2dd-5abe-5126-a600-a2b2dabdd536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251303} {:answer/id #uuid "74f3bd52-f1b5-5718-8268-e7a05e155702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251517} {:answer/id #uuid "8e91221e-72ff-5394-acc0-2c9ad06daba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251763} {:answer/id #uuid "f71d353b-a226-57e2-87d0-4fab47273cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251763} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a01557-5575-5007-a7d0-9ad8e1783ed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd2cfbb0-58d5-5839-b3b9-0140d20d0785" :question/math-academy-id "q-117749" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=6.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58561010-2ffb-5211-87c7-54354608cee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251278} {:answer/id #uuid "a92087be-a8aa-5c45-a599-91ea748bb8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252017} {:answer/id #uuid "c77bd7dc-c478-5f12-8e18-c9e5f5a2c95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252317} {:answer/id #uuid "ebaee17c-3905-5a18-8105-bf75cf8d3e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252619} {:answer/id #uuid "ebba7293-bebd-5c90-9b38-7f5ae167ad8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252619} :answer-field/id #uuid "a745d458-39b5-5d01-99cd-f164388d3a67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dfe6b278-448e-5b3e-ae3a-4c20840dfd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-117751" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=4\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2935cc2d-27c9-58b3-96a6-79c8386f3a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250904} {:answer/id #uuid "54137cf8-14cc-55d2-a3b0-586abb911a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251241} {:answer/id #uuid "80c578dc-923e-50b4-8174-f24b0bd436b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251638} {:answer/id #uuid "92a04e97-de9c-5fe7-b1bc-3c133f09c650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251808} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ed5eb0-3633-589d-acca-01d3eccf8929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251808} :answer-field/id #uuid "27623253-c44d-540a-b82d-ad67ac207865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b44fb065-7fc5-53f5-aa2b-171c7b74a7ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-117752" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}=12\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6e08f5-1299-5e6d-86b8-3f13d5d07062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251047} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb8b4b5-3d71-5acd-8e65-9d9908abc3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251075} {:answer/id #uuid "406eb494-4c42-5aa8-bce9-ecdb47a4fbea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251090} {:answer/id #uuid "b204afd2-5ee9-55c2-b8ea-43671338da79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252109} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fcb2fa-a77a-5a2b-a0df-705b56755327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251075} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b5e30a9-a960-5527-8571-bc85e85fe568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12dce0a0-75bf-584e-b4ec-b1bfde99680c" :question/math-academy-id "q-117803" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are **local minimums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$  For now, we're interested in the local minimums (not local maximums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -3+2 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-1$ at its local maximum points."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07787219-3aec-5958-9d79-2813628d4749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250616} {:answer/id #uuid "17b2af29-34f9-5189-9704-a9497fc2c1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250763} {:answer/id #uuid "783741c7-38de-5f40-8e21-7880f182c7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251548} {:answer/id #uuid "ac27db73-d3f2-5882-8eea-d4d9cb13da9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252048} {:answer/id #uuid "f2068b5c-e49e-557b-abb6-30a62bbdf25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252670} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa4cd93-bce2-5dac-8394-1309b6d408e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "334a5b82-93d0-57d2-8586-f1e8ca26194d" :question/math-academy-id "q-117804" :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\n$$ at its local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and adding $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & f(x)\\ge \\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{11}{3}$ at its local minimum points."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ef0ad4-504c-5ff5-b6b1-d2d3e980875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250635} {:answer/id #uuid "298bec1a-ff16-5540-a476-6b3fca320aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250908} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc5b675-6831-54be-906b-a9a1b71d2899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250944} {:answer/id #uuid "885e749a-e263-5b04-8671-ccd884576931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251703} {:answer/id #uuid "a365c952-0f98-5c32-87e5-0e635c62c1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250944} :answer-field/id #uuid "94db1709-d36e-5a1b-b01a-c474544f7620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "97fbbd90-72d5-5259-afd8-7a25639443c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-117805" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -4 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -4+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-3$ at the local maximum points."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15587c71-a1fb-523d-80ad-83e418f62f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={720}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250737} {:answer/id #uuid "16649bec-ba75-5e16-aba9-b7e5d9c0c67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250743} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5cbab5-9d4b-51af-b546-54c855288d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251186} {:answer/id #uuid "7f549b15-4e9d-5667-986e-8eb23eb54b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251622} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6d2104-e6f2-5693-bc52-2a24e7330cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250743} :answer-field/id #uuid "643d1b4e-a428-5f4e-b1de-a027b424fc54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0fbfbab-de05-59b4-b74a-867ddf558108" :question/math-academy-id "q-117809" :question/problem "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =4\\cdot {60}^{∘}+4\\cdot n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16da4dc7-6b4f-51ba-be04-2f71eb0c0e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250751} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3ec49c-0534-5804-8706-431242e89f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251180} {:answer/id #uuid "5af4ecb2-05e4-5fb0-9411-234348471c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251305} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffd704d-3d2e-5e6c-8630-efcb35a0ff49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251931} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ebd7cd-cfca-53ee-af39-cb153341eec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250751} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe48b54-f9a6-5763-be9b-5602877fb458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2ced06e-491e-5c02-9d8a-f6fcb8e39a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117851" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e5d6c9ce3198b5950c8a5396cf5500f4bddd232fbe44613611873c294dda6ea.png)\n\nThe line $3y+x=12$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+x & =12 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-0 & =12 \\\\ 3y & =12 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(12,0)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{12+0}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(6,2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(6,2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(12,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(6-12)}^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-6)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(6,2)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{10},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10077df9-a9a1-57a7-954a-0da2fbc15f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250693} {:answer/id #uuid "148d8fd0-a5d6-5959-b399-3819299189df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250732} {:answer/id #uuid "1979d8a2-ad6b-5c6a-aa0e-b9646d43d851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250775} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9d907e-81fd-556f-b931-d084995ea7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250805} {:answer/id #uuid "52bb5853-71ac-59d7-8798-850af855b804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250805} :answer-field/id #uuid "02fb79c0-8283-5202-bc70-9458092ba008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "195e8e21-ea85-5ab6-a113-abb18c6e98d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-117857" :question/problem "![](images/51/51d811f0b628a1854542d85954c26ca4658602cfcf7c207fbcfdb41115933139.png)\n\nThe line $4y+x=-8$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)+x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-0 & =-8 \\\\ 4y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(-8,0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{-8+0}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-4,-1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(-8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(-4+8)}^{2}+(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-4,-1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{17},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-(-4))}^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220f1f72-5993-5355-928d-610445b3ed08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250843} {:answer/id #uuid "3be885d3-e88e-54bb-a8e6-482e123cc00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251054} {:answer/id #uuid "e6174268-79ef-50fb-8545-ca02d9f8549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252568} {:answer/id #uuid "e9904d8b-ac81-5503-a083-08837b2b1210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252598} {:answer/id #uuid "efacbdff-57eb-5f3d-aa57-648622f32e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252650} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e62144-043a-5e64-91ce-e18508447330" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c63574e-df7e-5af6-a537-b5b20d65f6d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117859" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c100b27df3e4d9b23846961ad5d168fbe6f0eecd94ab8a9b99f51e82bca064a3.png)\n\nThe line $2y-3x=-24$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-3x & =-24 \\\\ -3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3(0) & =-24 \\\\ 2y & =-24 \\\\ y & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(8,0)$ and $(0,-12)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8+0}{2},\\frac{0-12}{2})=(4,-6).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(4,-6)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(4-8)}^{2}+(-6-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(4,-6)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{13},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{13})}^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1382a0da-6af2-58cd-a188-1d157bd20cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250722} {:answer/id #uuid "255f49c4-fc05-50ed-8b6d-c7d105680f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250875} {:answer/id #uuid "70d6cf7f-09b3-54e7-91e5-21216ebc3505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251475} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7c0e22-c4ef-5f16-9e47-d3a2ab3e0908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251770} {:answer/id #uuid "958902e2-d4fc-583d-abca-b3667fecc3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251838} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd471e5-3d02-59ea-ae43-9700669b6052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3de18967-1c79-524e-b070-80260fcd64fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117888" :question/problem "![](images/58/5819e0605dacfb190c2767c4f50a671eb592832aa6a3a1970a8bcdff2790fd19.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/ad/ad5bf0001a37cf5bebf3288ffbf1fa6bb69747f28f8a81d08d0ffab8223e1707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{21}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{21}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{21}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08bb1537-d88e-5b3f-911a-ebda185d71d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250631} {:answer/id #uuid "154d1a5b-94de-5332-826d-f7085ae1bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250735} {:answer/id #uuid "6982de92-482e-5773-8de9-08b335a18349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251422} {:answer/id #uuid "8612ead4-47dc-5874-b6e3-9dfd76a3c1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251675} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9bf3b6-8f0d-5e32-8900-ae60d369c4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250735} :answer-field/id #uuid "5303117c-9cc9-5aa9-a8ac-5b23f63b8352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e0561fe-63de-5941-8751-b8579e1f5296" :question/math-academy-id "q-117890" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d54c590d8d64670c0fb90baea864d154cb4c59cd69d7f57234bfd8a5c4a3bfe7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/33/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle 0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bcb0260-5769-5c20-88aa-a4f01f0ed3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251052} {:answer/id #uuid "605d6a7a-183a-572b-88b6-7f2f4255e6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251341} {:answer/id #uuid "645de680-5d7f-5ab0-a739-68065e200b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251374} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5d31a5-d46b-5fe9-a177-ab14a5602ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251890} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a9237f-441e-59ec-bbfc-da0afdb86299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251341} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8963d4e-4e20-5bae-a6bb-d591908baaca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "68a48b4f-4833-5c9d-96bb-4f430bff0952" :question/math-academy-id "q-117891" :question/problem "![](images/43/4374d7ede23ffbb78ed861d8ebdf116126c546490a87771505cfbe121b973247.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/ca/cac3ea6b8409290b6716a194325a885672e06572c1f8a8b63b48b6807f514d42.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26edb091-0b96-5397-9ba6-494835314768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250887} {:answer/id #uuid "5671d7ce-bf50-5c7c-b549-0d9d600fc956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251260} {:answer/id #uuid "658b9da1-a232-5391-a017-472bc560f421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251385} {:answer/id #uuid "c48bacda-3c46-5572-8209-c6bc50dcffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252294} {:answer/id #uuid "c5eeb26e-9198-56fc-b527-7d717763d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251385} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eea4f01-25e6-54fa-bcd8-b7c4d33ce0c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1bb6042-8ea9-56a9-af39-690a38b89fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-118075" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb13da8-7810-5c89-bfe5-cfd05edd7f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20xe" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250795} {:answer/id #uuid "6762d558-834e-5718-9f77-d36f7003c25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{20x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251402} {:answer/id #uuid "b641c6e7-a53f-57b9-be28-f4fe1f4831ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252157} {:answer/id #uuid "c80efe67-e261-5504-befb-6cc389986ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252327} {:answer/id #uuid "de816658-3425-5cd2-b6f9-e29388f5596e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251402} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d0e9a2-4b34-5118-9dda-d0eb67efc398" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c075cefc-4688-5882-8677-50d9b8a4d1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118089" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{20nx}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{20nx} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{20x} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{20x} \\\\  & ={e}^{20x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37725244-8e7d-5075-80ef-7b6bf5a1dbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "exy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251008} {:answer/id #uuid "41fc7021-61fd-56c8-ab99-39ebabdfa3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251103} {:answer/id #uuid "65870a10-fa73-5bb3-af78-9443d90fc53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251384} {:answer/id #uuid "90a56f2b-351b-5999-81f1-fd801ab32d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251785} {:answer/id #uuid "af2f2a35-38fb-52eb-bdd2-a7e8e12a28f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251785} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fefafe4-59ed-50f7-b86d-1c6cade14c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24044cb0-a390-54f5-a384-3ba8bf4f52b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118090" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nxy}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nxy} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{xy} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4498b675-43c3-5793-b8c9-fd822bdaaa19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251122} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6d59b4-11d6-5521-8805-c0d23ec13f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251204} {:answer/id #uuid "ce84ca5c-12e1-5f9c-8e96-2167a1a49769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252378} {:answer/id #uuid "d40efef2-1cef-59a5-a983-ed94a90b9102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\pi z}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252431} {:answer/id #uuid "fbca8839-e943-551e-9c84-9a7bcd5ae302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{z/\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252378} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b73cd5-d30f-583c-a11a-661fde1b71e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b144674-911f-5981-baf0-b97375ed0a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-118110" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{\\pi n}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{z}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{\\pi n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={({e}^{z})}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi z}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18683fc1-6e6f-552f-b3d8-1a598e647afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "d=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250768} {:answer/id #uuid "afd8104c-d61b-5874-bc43-96a15275c763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "d=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252087} {:answer/id #uuid "b3372c5e-1b3d-526f-a2ff-bdb9fc2ba5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "d=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252121} {:answer/id #uuid "c7189c96-8126-52ba-9ab5-81ef9869fbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252312} {:answer/id #uuid "db77b71c-5ce2-5ae9-9c3c-53b2ac47a7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "d=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252312} :answer-field/id #uuid "485e4678-0882-55ac-8cb1-e26d4e0dafbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8477c0d3-7e97-5832-b4f8-9e356450f509" :question/math-academy-id "q-118114" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{{d}^{2}-3d}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(d,\\,d(d-3),\\,d-3)=d(d-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $d=0$ and $d=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ d(d-3)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}) & =d(d-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4d(d-3)}{d}+\\frac{6d(d-3)}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2d(d-3)}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{d}\\cdot (d-3)}{\\cancel{d}}+\\frac{6\\cdot \\cancel{d(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d(d-3)}} & =\\frac{2d\\cdot \\cancel{(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d-3}} \\\\ 4(d-3)+6 & =2d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $d,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(d-3)+6 & =2d \\\\ 4d-6 & =2d \\\\ 2d & =6 \\\\ d & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $d=3$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0801c677-febc-5048-b4a9-bd16e29096aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250620} {:answer/id #uuid "259a4b59-97e1-5b2a-ae19-ec0054e36543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250878} {:answer/id #uuid "9b65b064-032f-5a74-8b4d-b5fd5feeec61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251892} {:answer/id #uuid "def82426-7548-5608-afb8-c119d37b39c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252518} {:answer/id #uuid "f83cbd43-a1c5-5373-b93c-4ff388a4a312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252518} :answer-field/id #uuid "6942e994-071a-50b2-a350-0106e069e5db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "686ef7d4-296f-572a-a3ca-d6455dccd76c" :question/math-academy-id "q-118117" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}+4v}=\\frac{3}{v}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}=\\frac{3}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(v+4,\\,v(v+4),\\,v)=v(v+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $v=0$ and $v=-4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ v(v+4)\\cdot (\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}) & =v(v+4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v(v+4)}{v+4}+\\frac{12v(v+4)}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3v(v+4)}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v\\cdot \\cancel{(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v+4}}+\\frac{12\\cdot \\cancel{v(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v(v+4)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{v}\\cdot (v+4)}{\\cancel{v}} \\\\ 6v+12 & =3(v+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $v,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6v+12 & =3(v+4) \\\\ 6v+12 & =3v+12 \\\\ 3v & =0 \\\\ v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $v=0$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba949de-81f6-54a0-8f19-c9fed59db56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251051} {:answer/id #uuid "763eccac-cf6b-548f-9e91-96b068420c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251525} {:answer/id #uuid "85d973e6-d911-51db-a990-b05e52077b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251674} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5e0cde-460b-5ce5-b7b3-75cfdfb23cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252062} {:answer/id #uuid "f73b5a87-92e7-5417-92ca-a4a93b3aa7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251051} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5e003f-a698-53ab-98d2-6521927c967e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d3e07973-1de0-5078-b548-53734ee05751" :question/math-academy-id "q-118123" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nWe make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z-1,\\,2z,\\,z(z-1))=2z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ 2z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}) & =2z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{10z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ 10z-10 & =z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10z-10 & =z-1 \\\\ 9z & =9 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b0138d-2866-5258-a4f6-9d837b99c527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250902} {:answer/id #uuid "7df1cd86-1b4f-5097-844e-cfce187ea242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251611} {:answer/id #uuid "8516da4a-4c43-57cc-b052-86c19c742adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251669} {:answer/id #uuid "f8904191-cbf8-5585-8d36-47d440dcd2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252730} {:answer/id #uuid "fa482220-9ab9-568b-b2b4-0a49a81fe20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252745} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a5ab9f7-7762-59d6-b037-5dbf0a82393d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44d44261-a717-51f0-9510-f6aa6afd3eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118128" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-6n}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-6n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-6} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-6} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-6} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f142a76-4d04-574a-bc79-9b28e54825e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250819} {:answ