wer/id #uuid "e155763f-096b-59b4-8147-c73fc9e34b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252537} {:answer/id #uuid "fabb789b-3482-52ab-9a7f-8ef7da12c6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252751} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5f0b3d-ce51-536e-ba60-d221656b3eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252751} :answer-field/id #uuid "4280e3c6-601d-57e2-b757-e5c26914fd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5be88614-2f37-5e45-8420-0b1cfbd655e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118166" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}=\\frac{1}{b-3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(b,\\,b(b-3),\\,b-3)=b(b-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $b=0$ and $b=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)} & =\\frac{1}{b-3} \\\\ b(b-3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}) & =b(b-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{b-3} \\\\ \\frac{b(b-3)}{b}-\\frac{7b(b-3)}{b(b-3)} & =\\frac{b(b-3)}{b-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{b}\\cdot (b-3)}{\\cancel{b}}-\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{b(b-3)}}{\\cancel{b(b-3)}} & =\\frac{b\\cdot \\cancel{(b-3)}}{\\cancel{b-3}} \\\\ (b-3)-7 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $b,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(b-3)-7 & =b \\\\ b-10 & =b \\\\ -10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above resulted in a false statement, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124692d9-2af8-525e-a8f7-0c0037843c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250710} {:answer/id #uuid "55afd260-1e85-5a0d-b412-c651e0a070e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251253} {:answer/id #uuid "a90d768c-39f5-5195-b7df-4cd0823405b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252016} {:answer/id #uuid "d5013fd4-24a1-56c9-bf99-8d299ccf2ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252437} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb0d9e6-f8e2-561b-aafc-8cf0b4689e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252626} :answer-field/id #uuid "867f9da4-8500-541b-9bcc-84efa5dc1174" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "095a0a7e-34d8-54e9-b5ce-745cce9de220" :question/math-academy-id "q-118173" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}-r}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(r(r-1),\\,r-1,\\,3r)=3r(r-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $r=0$ and $r=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1} & =\\frac{1}{3r} \\\\ 3r(r-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1}) & =3r(r-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3r} \\\\ \\frac{3r(r-1)}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{3r(r-1)}{r-1} & =\\frac{3r(r-1)}{3r} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{r(r-1)}}{\\cancel{r(r-1)}}-\\frac{3r\\cdot \\cancel{(r-1)}}{\\cancel{r-1}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{3r}\\cdot (r-1)}{\\cancel{3r}} \\\\ 3-3r & =r-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3r & =r-1 \\\\ 4 & =4r \\\\ r & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $r=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a984ec-000a-5987-a4ce-bb056594af64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "h=\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250557} {:answer/id #uuid "0c43ebf3-35d4-534f-87da-c5d3cba5fb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "h=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250656} {:answer/id #uuid "c25e5c91-b142-5aa7-9e94-6e7c3c24a68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "h=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252269} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fbba0b-53af-5587-8013-507685be1789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252289} {:answer/id #uuid "d30cb9b5-398a-504a-98c1-e5ef80a8674d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "h=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252289} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b09268a-2ef6-508c-8479-4c169f2b0516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aeddba54-5344-5f11-999d-95a273629db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118179" :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}+6h}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(h,\\,2h(h+3),\\,h+3)=2h(h+3).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ 2h(h+3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}) & =2h(h+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2h(h+3)}{h}-\\frac{2h(h+3)}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{2h(h+3)}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{h}\\cdot (h+3)}{\\cancel{h}}-\\frac{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}}{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}} & =\\frac{2h\\cdot \\cancel{(h+3)}}{\\cancel{h+3}} \\\\ 2(h+3)-1 & =2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(h+3)-1 & =2h \\\\ 2h+6-1 & =2h \\\\ 5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above resulted in a false statement, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6b342a-a7a5-561e-8f88-c3577522ff35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "570" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250646} {:answer/id #uuid "9d53eb6d-2f47-5435-92a8-6292a6713441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251910} {:answer/id #uuid "ad024548-ffea-5c8b-b659-3a0a9e598be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252059} {:answer/id #uuid "d522f8f5-a2db-5789-ad0b-62f3927c12ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252438} {:answer/id #uuid "f301621d-4c7f-5009-b76f-8a4b486f691f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251910} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c600ced-cc57-5258-b0b3-4c8596372824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b74d9046-36ae-5245-9ae7-2e1b28efe2e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-118556" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of chimpanzees in a Brazilian forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(1-\\frac{y}{500}),\\,y(0)=70,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible chimpanzee population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(1-\\frac{y}{500}) \\\\  & =y\\left(\\frac{500-y}{500}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{500}(500-y) \\\\  & =0.002y(500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $500,$ and we conclude that the number of chimpanzees cannot exceed $500.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ec7e2c-b0c1-5bd7-a12d-84ca400f61d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250708} {:answer/id #uuid "25fb9dc6-c258-5f89-afdb-545f7c00552c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250880} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd821bd-285e-5555-ba28-399ca5e97411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251058} {:answer/id #uuid "64118a44-f076-5605-988f-99284c490b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251373} {:answer/id #uuid "eb52a326-7d0f-5499-b88c-d9871834675d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "465" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250708} :answer-field/id #uuid "08033ad7-1d7f-5771-b794-5a4571be85ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e169e8f-e056-58a3-8af0-53918e153ecf" :question/math-academy-id "q-118561" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of sloths in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}),\\,y(0)=135,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible sloth population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}) \\\\  & =0.3y\\left(\\frac{600-y}{300}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.3y}{300}(600-y) \\\\  & =0.001y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of sloths cannot exceed $600.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2796c11a-4fa6-505d-9e0a-aa25efdb9c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250892} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec32c39-76e1-531c-a980-52d6069a233f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251335} {:answer/id #uuid "946eb912-f177-51c6-8055-52cb6bf80ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251830} {:answer/id #uuid "9c077374-79f0-523e-825a-f5521b2be909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251898} {:answer/id #uuid "bd295589-d9fe-5cee-b9fd-e8e6c502b237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252223}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251335} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8899cbd-a6dc-541a-a768-564111ff4288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bfa67a4d-773b-5a56-87ac-2e520036ce73" :question/math-academy-id "q-118618" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb5142f6-3668-5c25-bd3b-ad8d86c32b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448060} {:answer/id #uuid "7f997281-b88c-5dd5-bfb8-151c4ca4f3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448060} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c97076-8571-5bd9-b9cc-05ead189c06a" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a8cf32-8c05-57f0-b07c-8619e74a5e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448062} {:answer/id #uuid "2190c7c9-638d-5cac-b33b-bf19444c205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448063} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4d3171-d27d-5383-84f5-fff37db3a18f" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d086c6fe-ca1b-589b-9bf5-edc298cbf2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448064} {:answer/id #uuid "4c940020-f387-5c2f-8a62-86f9ec0bd046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448065}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448065} :answer-field/id #uuid "29dae20b-066b-57e8-839e-ee1a6bc08141" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "260fa3cd-f59d-50c6-a56e-8cfbd29393f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118755" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868fce7e-dc8d-506d-8541-a2b33751a81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448066} {:answer/id #uuid "93200da6-dc06-5778-8c75-662454876dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448067} :answer-field/id #uuid "6008c1c7-0c91-5c4f-b77d-3d2001714e26" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da1f27e2-e43a-595a-af92-12d42e44e236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448068} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a72708-a8dd-5f39-8ec8-0aa6abdf7840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4664ca-f99b-51de-bc33-708da1a4905c" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db0002c-4097-593b-977f-e88306dd33de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448070} {:answer/id #uuid "5820192e-8f09-59ad-9b73-592b6892f4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448071} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2690eb-e811-51ff-94f1-5c1490a665aa" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "96df78fc-91b5-5b18-90a4-e309d8513bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118761" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f1b0944-5746-51e3-95af-0c78d353b0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251078} {:answer/id #uuid "5483cc68-a72b-52f6-99ad-108af7c29b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251245} {:answer/id #uuid "712f686d-666e-5b50-837d-069f1c8844f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251478} {:answer/id #uuid "831c3d8d-8d9b-5446-847d-4a9578ff7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251659} {:answer/id #uuid "da79cc7e-e889-5625-9d36-6e8c5b69befe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251659} :answer-field/id #uuid "6264318d-7488-52ce-83d2-d05d6144f903" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64a6785a-7bbf-5956-bb67-37d306305466" :question/math-academy-id "q-118776" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=3+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{u}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})}\\right)|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2(3+0)}-\\frac{1}{2(3+{a}^{2})}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1680ffe0-948b-501f-aa4e-8c1642a5f303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250746} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb63dde-033e-59c9-8eb0-adb6b20d7c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251308} {:answer/id #uuid "715c410e-89fa-5c4c-ad41-a609422e7afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251482} {:answer/id #uuid "71839bd8-a676-51fc-bf9c-fcb7e22c2924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251483} {:answer/id #uuid "e35d940c-f7c4-5e91-aecb-b5feef0b625e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252553}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250746} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd025b40-5e66-5f9a-97e2-c4ec2e3a503d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "21a2ef07-2c09-57fc-ace5-9d591df7b8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-118777" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{3}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot [0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dac0b4b-3090-53d2-b003-7424d820c417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251064} {:answer/id #uuid "8c738aeb-f549-5507-b9ba-9642239886c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251738} {:answer/id #uuid "8d46f9cb-4b03-568c-b48c-0fc01fb5c082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251747} {:answer/id #uuid "9b025967-5a3f-5131-848c-0cd2325bce02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251887} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6071b7-c8e8-59d2-970e-4eb7a07c9776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251064} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c035291-788f-5932-9e15-2a5c7642a8e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0a2dfb2-a8f3-515c-8e4f-d42e9099dd28" :question/math-academy-id "q-118860" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{4}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{4}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{3\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2628def8-9645-51de-9e8f-3993f1de42dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250883} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2afe8b-693c-5e5d-8ae5-7dd6c9ecac38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250948} {:answer/id #uuid "7e485acb-f298-517c-be2f-3047650c561f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251613} {:answer/id #uuid "ce428001-7345-56f1-b009-0b23f9dd54d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252376} {:answer/id #uuid "d492b169-b001-5d91-9313-3a1cdca45303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252376} :answer-field/id #uuid "21d3ab40-897b-52d6-935d-dd5cec3b2f88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b105cb3a-38a2-5c0a-8e78-f3632457e0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-119032" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the derivative term is already isolated on the left:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222d3811-c9d6-5e7d-b85c-95aa3edf1f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}+C{e}^{-x}+Dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250846} {:answer/id #uuid "2aeffcd6-4797-59ec-860f-11aa268d3482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250918} {:answer/id #uuid "65573541-1d72-5f2c-8753-db5f49766c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+C{x}^{2}+Dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251383} {:answer/id #uuid "849fa2e5-06fb-57df-930c-0b02e73c0378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+C{e}^{-x}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251665} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1f009d-922c-586a-8478-6e8b8c14b8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250918} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdcccacb-a603-583d-bd6c-044e60d286f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31249dfd-dd0f-51ef-9508-c411dcb82eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119038" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=8{e}^{-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\int {e}^{-2x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x})+C \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{-2x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7a8ec6-3a7f-59a8-a36a-57b3c0e72d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251298} {:answer/id #uuid "7c79b537-ac7c-5f96-ac1d-d1860fe46a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251588} {:answer/id #uuid "bf97146c-f829-59ef-a419-71c2a32c9365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252244} {:answer/id #uuid "caaff75b-db1f-5d6d-9954-596535269be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252349} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a50774-5155-52c7-ab4f-ad427a04fce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251298} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dba1b47-1418-53b5-8e0b-75806451ee28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39f64cb7-5a70-5fc4-8565-289e61a98a66" :question/math-academy-id "q-119041" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =4\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52771b76-d1b4-51e6-ab25-23d4a36b8b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=16{e}^{x/2}+{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251227} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ba7691-e0f5-58b0-8306-ebfe9995ce2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252144} {:answer/id #uuid "d52fa157-a79b-5633-8056-0ad742ff72b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252440} {:answer/id #uuid "dad3e85e-1ff8-5404-b11a-42ecf755a4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=32{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252477} {:answer/id #uuid "e75f3c4e-0901-518a-8832-fdbd533170dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=32{e}^{x/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252477} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc11b9f-979a-5428-873c-d6f95fed2f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f306795-58e9-56b9-bfb8-50791b341767" :question/math-academy-id "q-119042" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6x=8{e}^{x/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=8{e}^{x/2}-6x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (8{e}^{x/2}-6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-6\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8(2{e}^{x/2})-6(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\  & =16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =16\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =16(2{e}^{x/2})-3(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})+Cx+D \\\\  & =32{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061e9049-685a-5e57-b12d-6b56030be323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250602} {:answer/id #uuid "42b59196-b9a8-573b-be83-9fd902357365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251109} {:answer/id #uuid "b5960315-6cae-5802-922d-5b341c99ee9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252150} {:answer/id #uuid "c18c981b-dd26-58f1-bd40-912972cd4a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252261} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e95291-17f3-571b-b000-bb1e43ebce08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250602} :answer-field/id #uuid "e27e7bd7-eaed-55b0-916c-a3dd3a5a5d99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f9dee74-c92e-5523-b157-7ba038c745c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119044" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{2}^{x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\left(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0877a493-110b-5bc5-b8f0-b95a212d57cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250624} {:answer/id #uuid "404b89d5-fb90-54b3-9fd1-b0ed48ad3aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)={x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251087} {:answer/id #uuid "7d24a901-b539-53de-9ab1-410020de269d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)={x}^{2}+2x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251597} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cb0cc0-4925-5d83-a3b2-07b01eb84c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2{x}^{2}+3x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252117} {:answer/id #uuid "c233f23d-d374-5888-ae7d-c2e80f985c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250624} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5f0d15-ef01-5c2b-8b45-e12411c1710b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70e058ec-5742-5754-884d-951409c47fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-119046" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)-4x=1$ that satisfies the condition $f(1)=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4x+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =4\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+x+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(1)=-1$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =2(1)^{2}+1+C \\\\ -1 & =2+1+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}+x-4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "778b9605-56f5-5801-9b37-1afd366faf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251540} {:answer/id #uuid "87ad96d2-dc83-5457-9f3f-9e32968fedd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251698} {:answer/id #uuid "89faada8-0f27-5ebf-91ff-42f76e4798d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251715} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbcadc7-9fad-5324-82df-926b4ba0aa6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251774} {:answer/id #uuid "926f3fa9-4b12-5f2a-a81c-eccdefd843ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251698} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f340d0e-66a6-54c5-a2ae-f77be864712f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "72e807f7-837a-5836-9313-38f63281c554" :question/math-academy-id "q-119199" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/11/11c74897fe914599a78c5997767bad83d13ddfb1e35ee66622d8880743688bad.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17cada-80e5-58f6-aeed-2e93b5243081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250667} {:answer/id #uuid "2afc783b-b9ed-5bd3-afa0-161d31d4cab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250919} {:answer/id #uuid "5611e50e-f57e-53b9-ad85-570dd238b4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251258} {:answer/id #uuid "6848ae3e-4124-5b9d-b387-6eb18ca003db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251408} {:answer/id #uuid "748c5c61-72ec-5a32-94cf-d53bf7b85eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250919} :answer-field/id #uuid "174407a9-f46d-55da-8697-5dbc5ce40bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6494dcea-ff4c-57dc-b8ab-235d70e87dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-119361" :question/problem "$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b4b6a2-fa4c-5800-a03c-dff3849ed336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡w-\\operatorname{cos}⁡w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250712} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb2d3b5-9f77-55ca-a49f-f753c8e38910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡w-\\operatorname{sin}⁡w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252219} {:answer/id #uuid "c94f2ecf-93ac-5340-87bc-eeb34a01cdd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡w-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252333} {:answer/id #uuid "e79cd7f0-cb9e-5c1e-81d7-3ea5800024fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡w-\\operatorname{cos}⁡w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252578} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3aec82-b34b-5e4c-93d4-b7250d5ad260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡w+\\operatorname{cos}⁡w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250712} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ed983a-988e-5ef0-83d9-e0d3d1d7aed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8e2c5aed-c37a-55b2-85b1-5c5820fa0a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-119485" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(w-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=w$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(w-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡w\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡w-\\operatorname{cos}⁡w).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d2902e-fe58-53b7-9bea-3127e6359fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461875} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ac292f-81f0-5439-8f10-2af989375112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461876} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eb618ec-27a1-5c30-9f69-da8678e0d24e" :answer-field/key "The point $(0,\\sqrt{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a721534-6ddd-57ca-b5b5-8ac253bdf5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461877} {:answer/id #uuid "022bbf55-1062-54d5-aec8-1dd458f8a82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461878}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461877} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4c8256e-8dfb-5fb9-b88c-844f3965001a" :answer-field/key "The point $(3,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462692} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68e47ca5-94e2-5e80-99e4-0cf645a6be09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461879} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff35a1a-1e27-5a4c-99ed-19f6834bf2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461879} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b6b0bfd-1f1e-5700-8876-61f888ab4569" :answer-field/key "The point $(-5,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "34508741-6c7f-54ff-a124-34cef40dd55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11967" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(-1,2),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a **clockwise** rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(0,\\sqrt{2})$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16151900-244f-5fa3-8f73-34dc1ffbe8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250742} {:answer/id #uuid "2523d48b-4a9a-5b1d-9609-7e8034036049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250869} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0d3f08-1c67-5237-9691-1901874fd496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251716} {:answer/id #uuid "b57b9c85-8925-5b42-905e-6abe2e616302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252147} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf395d5-7629-527f-b7c1-3fdbd410ebb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252147} :answer-field/id #uuid "d527deab-d128-5964-b2e9-88bf8d5e9b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f968e4e5-8d70-5545-88a7-87466239a48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119846" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of dolphins in a certain ocean follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}),\\,y(0)=43,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible dolphin population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}) \\\\  & =4y\\left(\\frac{1\\,400-y}{200}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{200}y(1\\,400-y) \\\\  & =0.02y(1\\,400-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,400,$ and we conclude that the number of dolphins cannot exceed $1\\,400.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d97584f-69ae-50f1-b569-00aac59e00db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250803} {:answer/id #uuid "2199af33-ca80-5220-96b4-31587e173074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250840} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcad8bb-b01d-58f1-ac7a-08e059eced7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250945} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ce2945-721b-587f-9dd1-3cf8600f2f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252165} {:answer/id #uuid "bb69b497-4881-511e-b142-4de53f2587c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250945} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc313da6-9cc1-5fe3-9af9-329c7ec66a1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "984868e6-9b0d-5753-97a4-a56b885226b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119948" :question/problem "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abdcc32-cc35-5670-9c83-c4cbe912eee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250650} {:answer/id #uuid "57edb35e-9370-5374-9e06-ccc1975123a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251271} {:answer/id #uuid "882f41e4-7a0d-5ac6-8538-7f29f7c131ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251701} {:answer/id #uuid "d69eebe4-9936-53c3-8b7b-de492f9722de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252454} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3248bd-252a-5193-96ee-ea1bc9f698ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250650} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9019c2-6685-5469-b58a-01ed2b00e34b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a84df43-c63e-5997-bb62-0b42cedd5faa" :question/math-academy-id "q-120017" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024f4b95-a6a5-5f2d-a751-2755ac5cce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250575} {:answer/id #uuid "0e20a623-1450-54d0-9595-8a7aa3109a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250675} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0ea007-0dad-5df4-93e4-28082fff80d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251033} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9f6191-c575-5400-985e-09588bd93769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251773} {:answer/id #uuid "adab3445-9088-5485-a515-0fc08abd7e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252067} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4e8fb7-0cf4-569d-9a43-083899802b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11defe63-9b06-5fba-87b6-5807bf8b34d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120018" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9091e145-d5e4-58db-9a8e-4ce061fcd0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251784} {:answer/id #uuid "aab6294d-67a7-58a9-aabd-cc0882409576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252031} {:answer/id #uuid "b330bc65-41bf-59e9-a350-941037eb07c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252120} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dbf917-e612-5719-99c8-5cfd2f0c0af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252691} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7138d3-5d87-57fc-b931-ce18940eaf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252691} :answer-field/id #uuid "db5fec58-f806-5ed1-8146-72c007a4dcf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83d0963d-a8db-5840-a576-ccf3c21b5f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-120020" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ in the numerator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f785b1-458a-5fb7-aba5-77cefec577f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250579} {:answer/id #uuid "49bb700e-a1de-516d-87ba-f936ef57ceae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251166} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8f3721-64a5-5d00-9f26-2a8bc5dfc279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251428} {:answer/id #uuid "b44be054-75f2-50ef-84f8-a25b52eb4a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252138} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f36356-34e3-5f0e-a2e1-dfe997539867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250579} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1fa67d6-78b4-56b2-a6da-1c23d4b075a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceef643d-5955-5dc4-9610-8fc14edd1b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120024" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in the numerator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fcc529-6327-5a47-a71b-9884c98ea937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250715} {:answer/id #uuid "452da317-cd5b-5e2c-a807-a5fb3eafe527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251127} {:answer/id #uuid "61086b08-503f-5afe-8d5f-93b7e8662a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251347} {:answer/id #uuid "b2646330-ca77-5a0d-b193-c06bcdc7a115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252111} {:answer/id #uuid "d26cc6ac-3efb-5846-8c19-760d22ea431d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251347} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f04de3e-9c49-5ec2-bb16-a4b47af64121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ad1365bd-d309-5764-8412-78c271945c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-120058" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57428bd5-b536-5679-aea6-fd81151b1b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461905} {:answer/id #uuid "eca1e859-1f88-5247-ad2a-b6421e23665a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461905} :answer-field/id #uuid "32e6e343-0561-57a5-bca5-94897a041baa" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e488bb-c9eb-5f1c-9403-74a0fb15149d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461907} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d9969f-476f-5ff9-80c3-e734d4ba0b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461907} :answer-field/id #uuid "65a0c5b6-3109-51ca-821e-2d69c40f39f0" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1cdc35e-ec49-5951-a0f1-9d4314c0e098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461909} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e49dca-fa51-5326-9677-f3a2d54d14d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461910} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ba20859-bde5-5414-ae2b-f393052e586d" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c84723b8-0d5e-5a26-bf52-a830d4f235da" :question/math-academy-id "q-120076" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fdb2ddd3d07537c1e3d0d22d435928f7cd11dc7a71c67f1bf45292c82a61ef7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/cf/cf2ba44d8cc21ade2204a3784af7d09b492843339a8b98c4cde741b30dfa54db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to \\pm \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04698c75-dc83-5fbb-8f53-9052909b1e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250585} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2c3035-0235-5603-a72a-eb385d8843e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250786} {:answer/id #uuid "6012bda1-9035-5aff-82de-2b534c046c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251340} {:answer/id #uuid "9aad87db-038e-549a-b557-3d3499e0f30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251882} {:answer/id #uuid "df46eab3-2fa6-5ef3-9539-002572011a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251340} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90d146b-981f-56ed-88e6-d9ffffc3ab01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "91b3ae38-a411-55e5-ba0c-b0976c1d82dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-120093" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{32}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{32})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right) & =4[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)]\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right) & =2[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)] \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217773cc-9a72-5309-917b-175c453ddfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250837} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc47fef-eeac-5dad-a55d-0f6242048a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251337} {:answer/id #uuid "75ffc259-508f-5bb1-98b6-4c82f16e3634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251523} {:answer/id #uuid "8a541a16-d054-5f31-9f18-54e78bfdbf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251720} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e95c7-d45f-56b8-b0c9-fc8dbcb94077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252319}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251720} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb8c2d27-0249-5da6-9044-57d8e8b49b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d0dc5da-5a41-5ef8-908b-57e6af4cb686" :question/math-academy-id "q-120171" :question/problem "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=4$ intersects the negative $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=4$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $y$-axis at the point where $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/08/0861ee15a875e00738a237d2788e26432c1c4c128ad7510a881f31aebaaff491.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee252b3-2fc9-58a1-a526-0ad834b9b6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250686} {:answer/id #uuid "18391112-3f3a-57a1-9bfe-ef48a937491a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250766} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9cae62-9c68-5e87-9549-65a72091d03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250813} {:answer/id #uuid "405c4f12-0925-5899-8b0c-bf237460ec93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251088} {:answer/id #uuid "988c1baf-6f97-581c-b7f2-f8c57f43988e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251863} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae027c21-9aa0-573c-bcf0-1d5cd80234b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03a455ba-2873-5368-831e-308d6a1a251d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12018" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-3.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x-3)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-(-3)\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3)+0 \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6f4f87-1ccd-589c-90e1-8890c55c15eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250932} {:answer/id #uuid "62a869bc-7ad8-5514-a78a-02dff1b40c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251359} {:answer/id #uuid "89286d84-c732-59b4-956d-104585bfdb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251711} {:answer/id #uuid "94083696-43f4-54f2-b3f6-a3866a469f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251825} {:answer/id #uuid "a5579b86-e0b3-519e-954e-6b1008604303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251978} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd003bc0-32be-5a18-932b-9a7f1e93dea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0eb4fa3e-52f2-52cc-a5b3-7f2b007d645a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120234" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}}\\right)| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191782ef-158c-5c61-8b5a-53889b26df66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250772} {:answer/id #uuid "27773dbc-82c6-5d97-8262-5945a24bc201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250891} {:answer/id #uuid "4683b3fe-eb8e-5220-9fa6-0a9afcd7c7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251140} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b8421d-9d4a-5d21-9ad8-60eec16fae69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252341} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fd4633-c96e-5bff-9e85-4b2076e4aa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251140} :answer-field/id #uuid "62588679-92ee-54c4-be84-9e822e9c62f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1855c488-31cc-5b55-93ba-bcc9b609f94f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120243" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-a,a),$ where $a$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-a)^{2}+{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{-a}\\right)| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0290f627-3c83-5afe-b636-ad528f0fe312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{5},-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253686} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2483de-bb9b-57f7-a79f-34885e11205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253803} {:answer/id #uuid "353dc5c0-d9b8-58b9-aa09-661aa2b47115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17